  1: # S02 Lanefield engine: the 6,144-lane world
  2: 
  3: Version 1.0.1, 2026-10-30. Depends on: S00, DESIGN.md sections 1, 6, 8, 12 (`world`). Blocks: S03, S05, S06, S07, S08, S10, S11.
  4: 
  5: ## 1. Purpose
  6: 
  7: One WebGL2 canvas, fixed behind the DOM, that draws all 6,144 lanes in one draw call and can morph them between four layouts (server, leaflink, sidequests, scatter) by changing four uniform weights. Scene specs add behavior on top (pulses, pins, trails); this spec owns the renderer, the data layout, the shaders, the clock, the camera, picking, the performance governor and failure handling. If this spec is built right, every scene is mostly a layout generator plus a little controller code.
  8: 
  9: ## 2. Public API (TypeScript, `src/world/lanefield.ts`)
 10: 
 11: ```ts
 12: export type LayoutId = 'server' | 'leaflink' | 'sidequests' | 'scatter';
 13: export type Regime = 'void' | 'paper' | 'field';
 14: 
 15: export interface Lanefield {
 16:   ready: Promise<void>;                       // resolves after layouts uploaded, shaders compiled, first frame drawn
 17:   travelTo(id: LayoutId, opts?: {instant?: boolean}): Promise<void>; // resolves when the 700ms travel ends (or immediately if instant)
 18:   setReducedMotion(on: boolean): void;        // switches render loop to demand mode and freezes ambient motion
 19:   setActivity(lanes: Int32Array, startSec: Float32Array, fadeAtSec: Float32Array, kind: 1 | 2, uPerSec: Float32Array): void; // S06 routes, S07 trails
 20:   clearActivity(kind?: 1 | 2): void;
 21:   now(): number;                              // world clock in seconds (the only clock; scene controllers use it)
 22:   screenToGround(xCss: number, yCss: number): [number, number] | null; // ray to plane y=0, world xz
 23:   worldToScreen(p: [number, number, number]): [number, number];         // CSS px
 24:   onFrame(cb: (tSec: number, dtSec: number) => void): () => void;       // scene controllers tick here
 25:   scene: THREE.Scene; camera: THREE.PerspectiveCamera;                  // controllers add pins, markers, words
 26:   dispose(): void;
 27: }
 28: export function createLanefield(mount: HTMLElement, opts: {seed: number; reducedMotion: boolean; initial: LayoutId}): Lanefield | null; // null when WebGL2 is unavailable
 29: ```
 30: 
 31: Expose a read-only debug view on `window.__lanefield` in every build (tests depend on it): `{laneCount, layoutHash: string (SHA-256 hex of the layout Float32Array), weights(): number[4], cameraPose(): {pos, target, fov, shift}, info(): {calls, triangles, textures, geometries}, frameStats(): {p50, p90, p95, count}, dpr(): number, memoryEstimate(): number (MB, method in S11 section 4), ready: boolean}`.
 32: 
 33: ## 3. Renderer setup
 34: 
 35: ```ts
 36: const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({canvas, antialias: false, alpha: false, depth: false, stencil: false, powerPreference: 'high-performance', premultipliedAlpha: true});
 37: renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
 38: renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping;
 39: renderer.setClearColor(groundColorForRegime, 1);
 40: ```
 41: 
 42: - Canvas: `position: fixed; inset: 0; width: 100vw; height: 100dvh; z-index: 0`. The DOM sits above at z-index 1.
 43: - Pixel ratio: `Math.min(devicePixelRatio, T.world.dprCap.fine or .coarse) * governorScale`, floor `T.world.dprCap.floor`.
 44: - No post-processing. No bloom, no vignette, no grain. Light comes from lane density and pulses (DESIGN section 3.3, no glow without a source).
 45: - Clear color is lerped per frame from the regime grounds by the same weights as the lanes, using the plain-ease curve over the travel duration (S03 section 4), never expo-out (a dark-to-paper flip under expo-out flashes).
 46: 
 47: ## 4. Data layout
 48: 
 49: ### 4.1 Layout texture
 50: 
 51: - `DataTexture(Float32Array(256 * 768 * 4), 256, 768, THREE.RGBAFormat, THREE.FloatType)`, `NearestFilter`, no mipmaps, `flipY false`, `needsUpdate` once.
 52: - Texel index for lane `i` (0 to 6143), layout `l` (0 server, 1 leaflink, 2 sidequests, 3 scatter), point `k` (0 to 7): `idx = i * 32 + l * 8 + k`; texel `(idx & 255, idx >> 8)`.
 53: - RGB: world position of the point. A: the lane's class id in that layout (same value at all 8 points).
 54: - Class ids (global): 0 server.lane, 1 server.converge, 2 leaflink.local, 3 leaflink.arterial, 4 sq.grid, 5 sq.outline, 6 sq.hatch, 7 sq.walkway, 8 sq.trail, 9 scatter.lane. Activity overrides: 10 leaflink.route, 11 sq.trailLit.
 55: - Style uniforms built from DESIGN classes: `uClassStyle[16]` vec2 (widthPx, alpha) and `uClassColor[16]` vec3 (linear RGB). Class colors: 0 and 1 signal; 2, 3, 10 ink; 4 to 8 chalk; 11 chalk core with signal rim (fragment, section 5.3); 9 signalDim.
 56: 
 57: ### 4.2 Activity texture
 58: 
 59: `DataTexture(Float32Array(256 * 24 * 4))`, one texel per lane at `(i & 255, i >> 8)`. R start time (s), G fade-start time (s), B kind (0 none, 1 route, 2 trailLit), A reveal rate in lane-u per second (negative means the path runs from u=1 toward u=0). `setActivity` writes texels and sets `needsUpdate = true` once per call (a 98 KB upload, at most a few times per second).
 60: 
 61: ### 4.3 Geometry
 62: 
 63: `InstancedBufferGeometry` with a 4-vertex quad: `position` attribute `(0,-1,0) (1,-1,0) (0,1,0) (1,1,0)`, index `[0,1,2, 2,1,3]`. Instance attribute `aSeg` (`InstancedBufferAttribute(Float32Array(43008 * 2), 2)`): for instance `n`, `aSeg = (lane = floor(n / 7), k = n % 7)`. `instanceCount = 43008`. `frustumCulled = false`.
 64: 
 65: ### 4.4 Layout generation (Web Worker)
 66: 
 67: `src/world/worker.ts` generates all four layouts into one transferable `Float32Array` and posts it with any per-scene side data (LeafLink graph, SideQuests marker positions and trail lane lists). It uses the seeded PRNG below and nothing else for randomness. Generators are specified in S05 (server), S06 (leaflink), S07 (sidequests), S08 (scatter). Every generator must emit exactly 6,144 lanes of 8 points; the worker asserts this and posts an error otherwise (the world then fails closed to the poster, S10).
 68: 
 69: ```ts
 70: // mulberry32, verbatim. The only RNG in src/world.
 71: export function rng(seed: number) {
 72:   let a = seed >>> 0;
 73:   return () => {
 74:     a = (a + 0x6D2B79F5) >>> 0;
 75:     let t = a;
 76:     t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1);
 77:     t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61);
 78:     return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
 79:   };
 80: }
 81: ```
 82: 
 83: Each layout gets its own stream: `rng(T.world.seed + layoutIndex * 1000003)`.
 84: 
 85: ## 5. Shaders (GLSL ES 3.00, `THREE.ShaderMaterial` with `glslVersion: THREE.GLSL3`)
 86: 
 87: Material: `transparent: true`, `depthTest: false`, `depthWrite: false`, `blending: THREE.CustomBlending`, `blendSrc: THREE.OneFactor`, `blendDst: THREE.OneMinusSrcAlphaFactor`, `premultipliedAlpha: true`. Three injects `projectionMatrix`, `modelViewMatrix`, `position` for `ShaderMaterial`.
 88: 
 89: ### 5.1 `lane.vert.glsl`
 90: 
 91: ```glsl
 92: precision highp float;
 93: precision highp int;
 94: precision highp sampler2D;
 95: 
 96: in vec2 aSeg;                 // (lane, k)
 97: uniform sampler2D uLayouts;
 98: uniform sampler2D uActivity;
 99: uniform vec4 uW;              // layout weights, sum 1
100: uniform vec2 uViewport;       // drawing buffer size, device px
101: uniform float uDpr;
102: uniform float uTime;
103: uniform vec2 uClassStyle[16]; // widthPx (CSS), alpha
104: uniform vec3 uClassColor[16];
105: uniform vec2 uFog;            // view-depth fade [start, end], server and scatter only
106: 
107: out float vSide;
108: out float vHalf;
109: out float vU;
110: out vec3 vColor;
111: out float vAlpha;
112: flat out int vLane;
113: flat out int vKind;
114: flat out vec4 vAct;
115: 
116: vec4 pt(int lane, int lay, int k) {          // 'layout' is a GLSL keyword
117:   int idx = lane * 32 + lay * 8 + k;
118:   return texelFetch(uLayouts, ivec2(idx & 255, idx >> 8), 0);
119: }
120: 
121: void main() {
122:   int lane = int(aSeg.x + 0.5);
123:   int k = int(aSeg.y + 0.5);
124:   vec3 p0 = vec3(0.0); vec3 p1 = vec3(0.0);
125:   float widthCss = 0.0; float alpha = 0.0; vec3 color = vec3(0.0);
126:   for (int l = 0; l < 4; l++) {
127:     float w = uW[l];
128:     if (w <= 0.0) continue;
129:     vec4 a = pt(lane, l, k);
130:     vec4 b = pt(lane, l, k + 1);
131:     p0 += w * a.xyz; p1 += w * b.xyz;
132:     int cls = int(a.w + 0.5);
133:     widthCss += w * uClassStyle[cls].x;
134:     alpha    += w * uClassStyle[cls].y;
135:     color    += w * uClassColor[cls];
136:   }
137: 
138:   vec4 act = texelFetch(uActivity, ivec2(lane & 255, lane >> 8), 0);
139:   int kind = int(act.b + 0.5);
140:   if (kind > 0 && uTime >= act.r) {
141:     int cls = kind == 1 ? 10 : 11;
142:     float on = kind == 1 ? uW.y : uW.z;          // routes exist only in leaflink, trails only in sidequests
143:     widthCss = mix(widthCss, uClassStyle[cls].x, on);
144:   }
145: 
146:   vec4 c0 = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(p0, 1.0);
147:   vec4 c1 = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(p1, 1.0);
148:   if (c0.w < 0.01 || c1.w < 0.01) { gl_Position = vec4(2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 1.0); return; }
149: 
150:   vec2 s0 = c0.xy / c0.w * 0.5 * uViewport;
151:   vec2 s1 = c1.xy / c1.w * 0.5 * uViewport;
152:   vec2 d = s1 - s0;
153:   float len = length(d);
154:   vec2 dir = len > 1e-4 ? d / len : vec2(1.0, 0.0);
155:   vec2 nrm = vec2(-dir.y, dir.x);
156: 
157:   float wDev = widthCss * uDpr;
158:   float drawW = max(wDev, 1.0);                  // never thinner than 1 device px
159:   float coverage = min(wDev, 1.0);               // sub-pixel lanes fade instead of shimmer
160:   float halfW = drawW * 0.5;                     // 'half' is reserved in GLSL ES
161:   float ext = halfW + 1.0;                        // 1px feather for analytic AA
162: 
163:   vec4 c = mix(c0, c1, position.x);
164:   c.xy += nrm * position.y * ext / (0.5 * uViewport) * c.w;
165:   gl_Position = c;
166: 
167:   vSide = position.y * ext;
168:   vHalf = halfW;
169:   vU = (float(k) + position.x) / 7.0;
170:   vColor = color;
171:   float depth = mix(c0.w, c1.w, position.x);
172:   float fogAmt = smoothstep(uFog.x, uFog.y, depth) * (uW.x + uW.w);
173:   vAlpha = alpha * coverage * (1.0 - fogAmt);
174:   vLane = lane;
175:   vKind = kind;
176:   vAct = act;
177: }
178: ```
179: 
180: ### 5.2 `lane.frag.glsl`
181: 
182: ```glsl
183: precision highp float;
184: precision highp int;
185: 
186: in float vSide; in float vHalf; in float vU; in vec3 vColor; in float vAlpha;
187: flat in int vLane; flat in int vKind; flat in vec4 vAct;
188: 
189: uniform float uTime;
190: uniform vec4 uW;
191: uniform vec3 uHot;            // signalHot, linear
192: uniform vec3 uRouteColor;     // ink, linear
193: uniform vec3 uTrailCore;      // chalk, linear
194: uniform vec3 uTrailRim;       // signal, linear
195: uniform vec2 uFirePeriod;     // seconds [min, max]
196: uniform float uFireDuty, uFireSpeed, uFireSigma, uSweepPeriod, uFirePeak;
197: uniform float uFadeSec;
198: uniform float uFrozen;        // 1.0 under reduced motion: pulses at fixed phases
199: 
200: out vec4 outColor;
201: 
202: float hash11(float p) { p = fract(p * 0.1031); p *= p + 33.33; p *= p + p; return fract(p); }
203: 
204: float fire(int lane, float u, float t) {
205:   float warp = float(lane >> 5);
206:   float sm = float(lane >> 7);
207:   float h = hash11(warp * 17.0 + 3.0);
208:   float period = mix(uFirePeriod.x, uFirePeriod.y, h);
209:   float phase = fract(t / period + h);
210:   float occ = step(phase, uFireDuty);            // 'active' is reserved in GLSL ES
211:   float sweep = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin(6.2831853 * (t / uSweepPeriod - mod(sm, 8.0) / 8.0));
212:   float head = fract(t * uFireSpeed + hash11(warp + 101.0));
213:   float dd = u - head; dd -= floor(dd + 0.5);
214:   float pulse = exp(-(dd * dd) / (uFireSigma * uFireSigma));
215:   return occ * pulse * (0.55 + 0.45 * sweep);
216: }
217: 
218: void main() {
219:   float edge = 1.0 - smoothstep(vHalf - 0.5, vHalf + 0.5, abs(vSide));
220:   vec3 col = vColor;
221:   float a = vAlpha;
222: 
223:   if (uW.x > 0.0) {                              // server pulses, weighted out in other layouts
224:     float t = uFrozen > 0.5 ? 0.0 : uTime;
225:     float f = fire(vLane, vU, t) * uW.x;
226:     col = mix(col, uHot, f);
227:     a = mix(a, uFirePeak, f);
228:   }
229: 
230:   if (vKind > 0 && uTime >= vAct.r) {            // routes (1) and lit trails (2)
231:     float rate = vAct.a;
232:     float travelled = (uTime - vAct.r) * abs(rate);
233:     float along = rate >= 0.0 ? vU : 1.0 - vU;
234:     float shown = step(along, travelled);
235:     float fade = vAct.g > 0.0 ? 1.0 - clamp((uTime - vAct.g) / uFadeSec, 0.0, 1.0) : 1.0;
236:     float on = (vKind == 1 ? uW.y : uW.z) * shown * fade;
237:     vec3 target = vKind == 1 ? uRouteColor : mix(uTrailRim, uTrailCore, edge);
238:     col = mix(col, target, on);
239:     a = mix(a, 1.0, on);
240:   }
241: 
242:   a *= edge;
243:   if (a < 0.003) discard;
244:   outColor = vec4(col * a, a);
245: }
246: ```
247: 
248: Color uniforms are raw sRGB components, not linearized: `new THREE.Vector3(r / 255, g / 255, b / 255)` from the DESIGN hex. `ShaderMaterial` output is not color-managed, so writing sRGB values and blending them in sRGB makes the canvas match CSS colors exactly (the clear color, set with `setClearColor(hex)`, is converted by three and reads back as the exact ground hex). Do not pass `THREE.Color` objects to these uniforms: they hold linear values and the lanes come out dark and oversaturated (seen in the verification render).
249: 
250: **Verified 2026-10-30:** both shaders compiled and rendered the server layout with zero errors in Chromium (SwiftShader WebGL2) with three 0.186.1, one draw call, 43,008 instances. Two GLSL ES reserved words (`layout`, `active`) in the first draft were caught there and renamed.
251: 
252: ## 6. Clock and render loop
253: 
254: - One clock: `now() = (performance.now() - t0) / 1000`. `uTime` is set from it each frame. Scene controllers read `now()` for activity timestamps so shader and CPU agree.
255: - Loop: `requestAnimationFrame`. Each frame: compute `dt`, run travel tween, camera, `onFrame` callbacks, update uniforms, render. No allocation inside the frame (preallocate vectors).
256: - `document.visibilitychange` hidden: stop the loop; visible: restart and reset `dt` to avoid a jump.
257: - Reduced motion: demand mode. Render once after `ready`, after each `travelTo`, on resize, and on interaction callbacks. `uFrozen = 1`.
258: - Shaders: compile every material at init with `await renderer.compileAsync(scene, camera)` before the first visible frame, including pins, markers and the troika text material (add one hidden instance of each so it compiles). Nothing compiles during travel or interaction (G-PERF checks `renderer.info.programs.length` is constant after `ready`).
259: 
260: ## 7. Tween, spring, damp (`src/world/tween.ts`, verbatim)
261: 
262: ```ts
263: // Cubic bezier solver (same method as WebKit UnitBezier). Returns y for x in [0,1].
264: export function cubicBezier(x1: number, y1: number, x2: number, y2: number) {
265:   const cx = 3 * x1, bx = 3 * (x2 - x1) - cx, ax = 1 - cx - bx;
266:   const cy = 3 * y1, by = 3 * (y2 - y1) - cy, ay = 1 - cy - by;
267:   const sx = (t: number) => ((ax * t + bx) * t + cx) * t;
268:   const sy = (t: number) => ((ay * t + by) * t + cy) * t;
269:   const dsx = (t: number) => (3 * ax * t + 2 * bx) * t + cx;
270:   return (x: number) => {
271:     if (x <= 0) return 0; if (x >= 1) return 1;
272:     let t = x;
273:     for (let i = 0; i < 8; i++) { const e = sx(t) - x; if (Math.abs(e) < 1e-6) return sy(t); const d = dsx(t); if (Math.abs(d) < 1e-6) break; t -= e / d; }
274:     let lo = 0, hi = 1; t = x;
275:     while (hi - lo > 1e-6) { const v = sx(t); if (v < x) lo = t; else hi = t; t = (lo + hi) / 2; }
276:     return sy(t);
277:   };
278: }
279: export const easeSignature = cubicBezier(...T.motion.easeSignature);  // expo-out
280: export const easePlain = cubicBezier(...T.motion.easePlainBezier);     // CSS 'ease' as a curve, for WebGL-side values
281: 
282: // Underdamped spring step response, 0 -> 1. zeta < 1.
283: export function spring(zeta: number, freqHz: number) {
284:   const w = 2 * Math.PI * freqHz, wd = w * Math.sqrt(1 - zeta * zeta), k = zeta / Math.sqrt(1 - zeta * zeta);
285:   return (tSec: number) => 1 - Math.exp(-zeta * w * tSec) * (Math.cos(wd * tSec) + k * Math.sin(wd * tSec));
286: }
287: 
288: // Frame-rate independent exponential smoothing toward target.
289: export function damp(current: number, target: number, halfLifeMs: number, dtMs: number) {
290:   return target + (current - target) * Math.pow(2, -dtMs / halfLifeMs);
291: }
292: ```
293: 
294: Test: `easeSignature(0.3)` is between 0.80 and 0.86 (this is what puts 80% of travel inside the first 210ms of 700ms). `spring(0.7, 3.2)` peaks between 1.040 and 1.052 and is within 0.01 of 1 after 0.6s.
295: 
296: ## 8. Camera (`src/world/camera.ts`)
297: 
298: - Pose `{pos, target, fovDeg, shiftNdc}` from `T.world.camera[layout][variant]`, variant `coarse` when `innerWidth < T.breakpoints.coarseOrNarrowBelowPx`, or when `(pointer: coarse)` matches and `innerWidth < T.breakpoints.desktopFromPx`; otherwise `fine`. Re-evaluate on resize and orientation change.
299: - Principal point shift (puts the subject beside the readout without cropping): after `camera.updateProjectionMatrix()`, set `camera.projectionMatrix.elements[8] = -shift[0]; camera.projectionMatrix.elements[9] = -shift[1]; camera.projectionMatrixInverse.copy(camera.projectionMatrix).invert();`. Positive `shift[0]` moves the image right in NDC.
300: - Travel: pose components lerp by `easeSignature(t)`; fov and shift too.
301: - Idle yaw: rotate `pos` about the vertical axis through `target` by `idleYawDeg * sin(2 pi t / idleYawPeriodSec)` for the active layout (weights blend the yaw amplitude during travel). LeafLink uses `leaflinkDrift` instead: translate pos and target together by `(amp.x sin(2 pi t/P), 0, amp.y sin(2 pi t/P * 0.7))`. Off under reduced motion.
302: - Pointer parallax (fine pointer only, off under reduced motion): yaw and pitch offsets up to `parallaxDeg` from pointer NDC, smoothed with `damp(..., parallaxDampHalfLifeMs)`.
303: 
304: ## 9. Picking
305: 
306: - `screenToGround`: unproject a ray from CSS px through the camera (account for the shift by using `projectionMatrixInverse`), intersect plane y = 0.
307: - `worldToScreen`: project with the shifted matrix to CSS px.
308: - Markers and pins are hit-tested in screen space: hit if the pointer is within `marker.hitRadiusPx` (22px, so a 44px target) of the projected center. No GPU picking. No raycasting against lane geometry.
309: 
310: ## 10. Performance governor (`src/world/governor.ts`)
311: 
312: - Keep a ring buffer of frame `dt` over `governor.sampleWindowMs`. Every window: if p90 is above `stepDownAboveMs`, reduce the DPR scale by `dprStep`, never below `dprCap.floor`. If p90 stays under `stepUpBelowMs` for `stepUpAfterMs`, raise by one step up to the cap.
313: - Lane count never changes. Classes never drop. The only quality lever is resolution. This keeps the anti-template invariant true on every device.
314: - `?perf=1` shows a monospace HUD in the top-right (fps, p90 ms, dpr, calls) and records `frameStats` to `window.__lanefield`; `?perf=1&dump=1` downloads a JSON of the last 600 frames.
315: 
316: ## 11. Failure handling
317: 
318: - WebGL2 missing (`canvas.getContext('webgl2')` is null) or `?world=0`: `createLanefield` returns null; S10 shows the poster and C-NOGL.
319: - Worker error or lane-count assertion failure: same as missing WebGL2, and `console.error('[lanefield] layout failed', detail)`.
320: - `webglcontextlost`: `preventDefault()`, stop loop, show the poster under the canvas. `webglcontextrestored`: re-upload textures from the cached Float32Arrays, recompile, resume. Test with `WEBGL_lose_context`.
321: - `dispose()`: dispose geometry, material, textures, troika text, renderer; remove listeners; terminate worker. The site never disposes in normal use (one world for the whole visit); `dispose` exists for tests and HMR.
322: 
323: ## 12. Kill criteria
324: 
325: - K-S02-1: lane count anything other than 6144, or more than one draw call for lanes.
326: - K-S02-2: any frame allocates (Chrome performance profile shows minor GC during a 10s idle on Server).
327: - K-S02-3: a shader program is compiled after `ready`.
328: - K-S02-4: lanes shimmer or crawl when the camera idles (sub-pixel handling broken): reviewer watches 10s of Server at 1x DPR.
329: - K-S02-5: any main-thread task over 50ms during startup or travel (long-task observer, S11).
330: - K-S02-6: the field reads as a circuit board to a reviewer who has not seen the PCB versions (blind check in S11).
331: 
332: ## 13. Receipts
333: 
334: | Test | Claim | How |
335: |---|---|---|
336: | T-S02-1 | 6,144 lanes, one call | `__lanefield.laneCount === 6144`; temporarily hide pins, markers and words via `?debug=lanesOnly`, then `info().calls === 1` |
337: | T-S02-2 | Deterministic layouts | `layoutHash` identical across two fresh loads and across Chromium and WebKit |
338: | T-S02-3 | Weights sum to 1 at every frame of a travel | sample `weights()` each rAF during `travelTo`; `abs(sum - 1) < 1e-4` |
339: | T-S02-4 | Easing math | unit tests of section 7 numbers |
340: | T-S02-5 | Principal shift | on Server fine pose, `worldToScreen(target)` x is within 2% of `0.5 + shift[0]/2` of viewport width (72% for shift 0.44) |
341: | T-S02-6 | No compile after ready | `renderer.info.programs.length` unchanged across 3 full travels |
342: | T-S02-7 | Context loss recovers | lose and restore context, `layoutHash` unchanged, frame renders, no console error except the one expected warning |
343: | T-S02-8 | Startup off the main thread | long tasks during `ready` all under 50ms (PerformanceObserver `longtask`) |
344: 
345: ## 14. Unknowns
346: 
347: U-S02-1: real-device frame times (Justin's phone) are not measurable from a cloud runner; S11 G-PERF-DEVICE needs one manual run. U-S02-2: troika `BatchedText` availability in 0.52.5; if absent, the six word meshes cost six draw calls (still inside `drawCallsMax` 12).
348: 
349: ## 15. Changelog
350: 
351: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
352: - 1.0.1, 2026-10-30: T-S02-5 corrected. The test point was focus (0,0,2.2), which is not on the view axis, so its projection is not 0.5 + shift/2 (measured 0.909/0.939, hand-computed 0.924). The test point is now target, which is on the view axis by definition and projects to exactly 0.5 + shift[0]/2. The parenthetical was also wrong: 0.5 + 0.44/2 is 72%, not 66%. The shift mechanism itself was already verified exactly by B2's target projection.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/specs/S08-scene-notfound.md">
 1: # S08 Scene: not found (any unknown path)
 2: 
 3: Version 1.0.0, 2026-10-30. Depends on: S02, S03, S04, S09, DESIGN `world.scatter`, `world.camera.scatter`. Blocks: S11.
 4: 
 5: ## 1. Purpose
 6: 
 7: An unknown address. The lanes have nowhere to go, so they scatter into short unrouted strokes floating in the void. The readout says so in one line and points at the keymap. Small, on-system, and the fastest way back is one key.
 8: 
 9: ## 2. Visual identity
10: 
11: Void ground, signal dim strokes at 22% alpha, 1px, scattered in a sphere of radius 8. Slow yaw. Nothing else. It must look unfinished on purpose: the absence of structure is the message.
12: 
13: ## 3. Layout
14: 
15: Generator (`src/world/layouts/scatter.ts`, worker, `rng(seed + 3 * 1000003)`): for each lane, a center uniformly inside the sphere of radius `scatter.radius` (rejection sample in the cube), a direction uniformly on the unit sphere, a length uniform in `segLength [0.2, 0.6]`; 8 points evenly spaced along the segment; class 9.
16: 
17: Camera: `(0, 0, 14)` looking at the origin, fov 40 (fine); `(0, 0, 18)`, fov 52 (coarse). No shift. Idle yaw `±4 deg` over 24s.
18: 
19: Readout: variant `server` (left column fine, top coarse), C-404-H1 as `<h1>` at `--jp-text-display-1`, C-404-P1 at `--text-body`. The wordmark is shown. The keymap shows no active scene item.
20: 
21: ## 4. Motion
22: 
23: Entry: travel to `scatter` (700ms expo-out) when reached by `popstate` to an unknown path; on a direct load it starts in place. Idle: yaw only. Reduced motion: static. `ArrowRight` or `1` goes to the server.
24: 
25: ## 5. Content
26: 
27: C-TITLE-404, C-404-H1, C-404-P1. HTTP status 404 is the host's job (`dist/404.html`, S03 section 7).
28: 
29: ## 6. Kill criteria
30: 
31: - K-404-1: any joke, illustration, or "lost in space" copy.
32: - K-404-2: the page is a dead end (keymap missing or inactive).
33: 
34: ## 7. Receipts
35: 
36: | Test | Claim | How |
37: |---|---|---|
38: | T-404-1 | Unknown path renders the scene | load `/nope`: H1 C-404-H1, `weights()` one-hot index 3, title C-TITLE-404 |
39: | T-404-2 | One key home | press `1`: URL `/`, server readout visible, no document load |
40: 
41: ## 8. Changelog
42: 
43: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/specs/S10-fallbacks-a11y.md">
 1: # S10 Fallbacks and accessibility: the site with the world turned off
 2: 
 3: Version 1.0.0, 2026-10-30. Depends on: S02 to S09. Blocks: S11.
 4: 
 5: ## 1. Purpose
 6: 
 7: The world is the signature, not the content. Everything a visitor needs (who Justin is, what he built, how to reach him) must work with JavaScript off, with WebGL2 missing, with reduced motion on, with a screen reader, with a keyboard only, and on a slow phone. This spec defines each degraded mode and the posters that stand in for the world.
 8: 
 9: ## 2. Modes
10: 
11: | Mode | Detection | Behavior |
12: |---|---|---|
13: | No JS | n/a | Prerendered HTML for each route (S03 section 7). Readout text, links, keymap as plain links. Poster image of the scene behind the readout (`<img>` in `#world` with `alt=""` because the region label carries the description). Sheets are unavailable without JS, so the prerendered HTML includes a `<noscript>` block below the readout with C-WHO-* and C-CON-* content as plain text and links (the email as a `mailto:` link). |
14: | No WebGL2 | `createLanefield` returns null, or `?world=0` | Poster per scene, crossfading with the readout on travel (reduced-motion timing). C-NOGL shown once as a supporting line under the readout. All interactions that are world-only (LeafLink offers, SideQuests walks) are hidden: `OfferButton` and marker twins are not rendered, and C-LL-SIM and C-SQ-HINT are not rendered. |
15: | Reduced motion | `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` via the standard listener (`src/lib/reducedMotion.ts`, verbatim from the repo's standard implementations); reacts live to changes | DESIGN section 6.4 table. The world renders in demand mode. |
16: | Reduced transparency or more contrast | `prefers-contrast: more` | Lane alphas multiplied by 1.4 (capped at 1) via a uniform scale, hairlines at 30%. Text colors unchanged (they already pass). |
17: | Save data | `navigator.connection?.saveData === true` | The world does not load; posters are used (same as no WebGL2, without C-NOGL; instead nothing extra is shown). |
18: | Context lost | S02 section 11 | Poster under the canvas until restored. |
19: 
20: ## 3. Posters (`tools/posters.mjs`)
21: 
22: At build time, run the built site in Playwright Chromium (`executablePath: '/opt/pw-browsers/chromium'` in cloud runners if the bundled one is missing) with `?posters=1`, which: forces reduced motion (the frozen diorama), hides the DOM chrome, waits for `__lanefield.ready`, and screenshots the canvas at 1440 by 900 (fine pose) and 390 by 844 at DPR 2 (coarse pose) for each of the four layouts. Encode as WebP quality 72. Write `public/posters/{scene}-{fine|coarse}.webp`. Budget: each under 90 KB (the frozen field compresses well; if over, lower quality to 60, never resize below the viewport). Serve with `<picture>` and a `(max-width: 767px)` media query. Posters load lazily (`loading="lazy"`, `fetchpriority="low"`) and never compete with the font or the H1 for first paint; in the normal path they are never requested at all (the world mount decides at runtime whether to insert the `<img>`).
23: 
24: ## 4. Screen readers
25: 
26: - The world container is `aria-hidden="true"`. Each scene's `<main>` contains a `role="img"` element with the scene's ALT string (S09) so the drawing is described once, in words, and only the drawing.
27: - Heading structure per route: one `h1` (the readout heading), sheets use `h2`.
28: - Travel announces the new scene H1 through the polite live region (S03).
29: - Marker twins: `aria-pressed` for claimed. OfferButton: plain button, no live announcement (the legend explains the drawing; spamming route announcements every second would be noise).
30: - The keymap uses `aria-current="page"` for the active scene and `aria-expanded` for panels.
31: 
32: ## 5. Keyboard
33: 
34: Every action has a key path: scenes `1 2 3` and arrows, panels `w c`, `Escape` closes, `Tab` reaches every link, the OfferButton and the three marker twins. Focus is always visible (`:focus-visible` ring from Astryx focus tokens). Focus never lands on the canvas.
35: 
36: ## 6. Kill criteria
37: 
38: - K-S10-1: any fact on the site that is reachable only through the world (every number and name in the scenes must also be in DOM text).
39: - K-S10-2: JS-off view of any route is missing the readout, the keymap, or a way to the email address.
40: - K-S10-3: axe reports any violation in any mode.
41: - K-S10-4: reduced motion still moves anything other than opacity crossfades.
42: 
43: ## 7. Receipts
44: 
45: | Test | Claim | How |
46: |---|---|---|
47: | T-S10-1 | No-JS complete | Playwright with `javaScriptEnabled: false` on all four routes: H1 present, keymap links present, `mailto:` link present, poster `<img>` present |
48: | T-S10-2 | No-WebGL path | `?world=0`: C-NOGL visible, no OfferButton, no twins, travel works with poster crossfade |
49: | T-S10-3 | Reduced motion is still | `reducedMotion: 'reduce'`: two screenshots 2s apart on each scene are pixel-identical |
50: | T-S10-4 | axe in every mode | axe on each route in normal, no-JS, no-WebGL and reduced modes: zero violations |
51: | T-S10-5 | Posters in budget | each poster file under 90 KB |
52: | T-S10-6 | Posters stay out of the normal path | normal load of `/` with WebGL2: zero requests to `/posters/` |
53: 
54: ## 8. Unknowns
55: 
56: U-S10-1: VoiceOver on iOS behavior with `BottomSheet` is Astryx's responsibility; check once on a real device (manual, S11 G-DEVICE).
57: 
58: ## 9. Changelog
59: 
60: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/specs/S11-performance-qa.md">
 1: # S11 Performance and QA: the gates
 2: 
 3: Version 1.0.1, 2026-10-30. Depends on: all specs. Blocks: ship.
 4: 
 5: ## 1. Purpose
 6: 
 7: Turn every claim in the package into a check that passes or fails. A gate is a named group of tests; BUILD-ORDER.md says which gates must be green at each step. "Looks good" is never a gate. The final gate set is the definition of done.
 8: 
 9: ## 2. Harness
10: 
11: - Playwright `1.64.1`, projects: `chromium-desktop` (1440 by 900, DPR 1), `chromium-phone` (390 by 844, DPR 3, `isMobile`, `hasTouch`), `webkit-phone` (same as phone on WebKit), `chromium-reduced` (`reducedMotion: 'reduce'`), `chromium-nojs` (`javaScriptEnabled: false`).
12: - Served from `vite preview` on the built `dist/` (never the dev server) so prerender, chunking and compression are real.
13: - Network for perf runs: Lighthouse mobile preset (simulated throttling, Moto G Power class).
14: - Every test writes its receipt to `qa/<gate>/<test-id>.json` (numbers, pass flag, timestamp, git SHA) and screenshots to `qa/<gate>/*.png`. `tools/qa-report.mjs` rolls them into `qa/QA-REPORT.md` with one line per test.
15: - WebGL in headless Chromium: launch with `--use-angle=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader` so the canvas renders in CI. SwiftShader frame times are meaningless for performance; use it for correctness only. Frame-time gates come from real devices (G-PERF-DEVICE).
16: 
17: ## 3. Gates
18: 
19: | Gate | Tests | What it proves |
20: |---|---|---|
21: | G-BUILD | T-S00-1 to T-S00-6 | Clean clone builds; generated files reproducible; lints catch violations |
22: | G-TOKENS | `tools/lint-tokens.mjs`, T-S01-2 | No value lives only in code; no stock Meta blue |
23: | G-CONTRAST | `palette-receipt.py`, T-S01-3, T-S01-4, T-SRV-2, T-LL-5, T-SQ-3 | Every text pair passes in every regime, including over the live scene |
24: | G-COPY | T-COPY-1 to T-COPY-3 | Every word is from the deck; no dashes, no banned items, no GPA, no phone |
25: | G-WORLD | T-S02-1 to T-S02-8 | 6,144 lanes, one call, deterministic, recoverable |
26: | G-TRAVEL | T-S03-1 to T-S03-8, K-S03-4 check | No reloads, deep links, history, interruptible, feedback under 250ms |
27: | G-CHROME | T-S04-1 to T-S04-7 | Keymap never wraps; targets 44px; axe clean; copy paths; focus |
28: | G-SCENES | T-SRV-*, T-LL-*, T-SQ-*, T-404-* | Each scene does what its spec says |
29: | G-HUE | T-SRV-5, T-LL-7, T-SQ-6 against `hueBudgetPct` | Hue discipline (DESIGN 3.3) |
30: | G-FALLBACK | T-S10-1 to T-S10-6 | No-JS, no-WebGL, reduced motion, posters |
31: | G-PERF-LAB | section 4 | Lighthouse and bundle budgets |
32: | G-PERF-DEVICE | section 5 | 60fps on a real phone (manual) |
33: | G-ANTI | section 6 | It does not look generated |
34: | G-CONTENT | section 7 | Every claim traces to the brief |
35: 
36: ## 4. G-PERF-LAB (automated, every build)
37: 
38: | Metric | Budget (DESIGN `budgets`) | Measured how |
39: |---|---|---|
40: | LCP | under 2000ms, element is the route's H1 | Lighthouse mobile, median of 3 runs, per route |
41: | TBT | under 150ms | same |
42: | TTI | under 3000ms | same |
43: | CLS | under 0.02 | same (font swap is the risk; see the fallback metrics in DESIGN 4) |
44: | Long tasks | none over 50ms from navigation start to 5s after `ready`, and none during 3 travels | `PerformanceObserver({type: 'longtask', buffered: true})`, Chromium desktop and phone projects |
45: | Render-blocking CSS | under 45 KB gzip | sum of gzip sizes of CSS in `<head>` |
46: | App shell JS | under 110 KB gzip | entry chunk plus its static imports |
47: | World chunk | under 220 KB gzip | the `world` manual chunk (three, troika, engine), loaded on idle |
48: | Font preload | under 50 KB | the 400 woff2 |
49: | Draw calls | at most 12 in any scene | `__lanefield.info().calls` |
50: | Programs | constant after `ready` | `renderer.info.programs.length` |
51: | GPU memory estimate | under 64 MB | textures (layout 3.0 MB, activity 0.1 MB, troika atlas) plus instance buffers plus drawing buffer `w * h * dpr^2 * 4 * 2`; computed by `__lanefield.memoryEstimate()` and logged |
52: 
53: ## 5. G-PERF-DEVICE (manual, required before ship)
54: 
55: Cloud runners cannot measure real GPU frame times. Before ship, one person runs this on a real phone (Justin's iPhone is the reference device; add any Android phone if available):
56: 
57: 1. Open the deployed preview with `?perf=1`.
58: 2. On each scene, idle 20s, then travel 1 to 2 to 3 to 1 twice, then on LeafLink tap 5 times, then on SideQuests walk all three trails.
59: 3. Tap the HUD to export (`?perf=1&dump=1` downloads the JSON). Attach the JSON to `qa/device/<device>-<date>.json` and record p50, p95 and the final DPR in `qa/device/README.md`.
60: 
61: Pass: p95 frame time at most 16.7ms on every scene and during travel, with the governor's final DPR at least 1.25 on the reference phone. If p95 fails at DPR 1.0, that is a kill (K-SRV-6 and friends): the fix is in the shader or overdraw, never fewer lanes.
62: 
63: ## 6. G-ANTI (taste gate, human-judged, scripted questions)
64: 
65: A reviewer who has not read the specs looks at six screenshots (three scenes at desktop and phone) and the ContactSheet next to Astryx's stock BottomSheet docs example, then answers in writing:
66: 
67: 1. Name one concrete way this does not look generated. Pass if the answer is specific (for example "the navigation is a terminal keymap" or "every scene is the same lines rearranged"). Fail if the reviewer cannot.
68: 2. Does any scene look like a circuit board, a matrix-rain screensaver, a Leaflet map, or a game HUD? Any yes fails.
69: 3. Does the contact sheet look like the stock Astryx example? Yes fails.
70: 4. Point at anything that glows without a source, any gradient, any card, any eyebrow, any stat. Any found fails.
71: 5. Read the copy aloud. Does any sentence sound like a landing page? Quote it; quoted lines go back to S09.
72: 
73: Then run the mechanical checklist from `baseline/ui-ux/ui-slop-patterns.md` section 6 and record each line as CLEAN or FLAG in `qa/anti/checklist.md`. One FLAG is fixed before ship.
74: 
75: ## 7. G-CONTENT (traceability)
76: 
77: `tools/trace-content.mjs` loads `src/copy.ts` and checks every ID whose S09 source is `BRIEF` or `MEM` against an allow-list of fact tokens extracted by hand from CONTENT-BRIEF.md, plus the MEM facts S09 names (the Codeberg URLs, `Hack New Paltz`, `24`, `MVP`), into `qa/content/facts.json` (names, places, dates, placements, stack items, URLs). Any capitalized word or number in a BRIEF/MEM string that is not in the facts list fails. Example: `C-LL-FACTS` contains `Week-long`, `four`, `Justin Poli`, `Julian Shuster`, `Devin Perez`, `Colton A.`, `2nd place`: all in facts. A string that grew a "50 users" would fail.
78: 
79: ## 8. Kill list (any one stops the ship, whatever else passes)
80: 
81: - A pulse, route or trail that is not on a lane.
82: - Any lane count other than 6,144.
83: - Any text under its contrast requirement, at rest or during travel.
84: - Any fact not traceable to the brief, or any item from S09 rule 4 shipped.
85: - Any document reload on scene change.
86: - Any page scroll.
87: - Feedback over 250ms on a keymap press.
88: - G-PERF-DEVICE p95 over 16.7ms at DPR 1.0.
89: - G-ANTI question 1 unanswerable, or any yes on question 2.
90: 
91: ## 9. Ship rule
92: 
93: All gates green, the kill list empty, G-PERF-DEVICE recorded, every UNKNOWN in UNKNOWNS.md either resolved or explicitly accepted by Justin in writing. Claude never deploys or merges: the build hands a commit SHA, the `dist/` byte counts and the QA report to whoever deploys.
94: 
95: ## 10. Changelog
96: 
97: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
98: - 1.0.1, 2026-10-30: facts list includes the MEM facts now that C-SQ-VENUE ships.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/tools/palette-receipt.py">
 1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
 2: """Palette receipt: OKLab distance of every DESIGN.md color to 51 reference blues, and WCAG contrast of every pair in contrastPairs.
 3: Usage: python3 -I spec/astryx/tools/palette-receipt.py spec/astryx/DESIGN.md   (exit 1 on any failure)"""
 4: import math,sys
 5: def s2l(c):
 6:     c/=255; return c/12.92 if c<=0.04045 else ((c+0.055)/1.055)**2.4
 7: def l2s(c):
 8:     v = 12.92*c if c<=0.0031308 else 1.055*c**(1/2.4)-0.055
 9:     return max(0,min(255,round(v*255)))
10: def hex2rgb(h): h=h.lstrip('#'); return [int(h[i:i+2],16) for i in (0,2,4)]
11: def rgb2oklab(rgb):
12:     r,g,b=[s2l(x) for x in rgb]
13:     l=0.4122214708*r+0.5363325363*g+0.0514459929*b
14:     m=0.2119034982*r+0.6806995451*g+0.1073969566*b
15:     s=0.0883024619*r+0.2817188376*g+0.6299787005*b
16:     l,m,s=[x**(1/3) for x in (l,m,s)]
17:     return (0.2104542553*l+0.7936177850*m-0.0040720468*s,1.9779984951*l-2.4285922050*m+0.4505937099*s,0.0259040371*l+0.7827717662*m-0.8086757660*s)
18: def oklch(h):
19:     L,a,b=rgb2oklab(hex2rgb(h)); return L,math.hypot(a,b),(math.degrees(math.atan2(b,a))%360)
20: def oklch2hex(L,C,H):
21:     a=C*math.cos(math.radians(H)); b=C*math.sin(math.radians(H))
22:     l=L+0.3963377774*a+0.2158037573*b; m=L-0.1055613458*a-0.0638541728*b; s=L-0.0894841775*a-1.2914855480*b
23:     l,m,s=l**3,m**3,s**3
24:     r=4.0767416621*l-3.3077115913*m+0.2309699292*s; g=-1.2684380046*l+2.6097574011*m-0.3413193965*s; bb=-0.0041960863*l-0.7034186147*m+1.7076147010*s
25:     inside=all(-0.0005<=x<=1.0005 for x in (r,g,bb))
26:     return '#%02X%02X%02X'%tuple(l2s(x) for x in (r,g,bb)), inside
27: def lum(h):
28:     r,g,b=[s2l(x) for x in hex2rgb(h)]; return 0.2126*r+0.7152*g+0.0722*b
29: def cr(a,b):
30:     A,B=lum(a),lum(b); A,B=max(A,B),min(A,B); return (A+0.05)/(B+0.05)
31: def de(a,b):
32:     x=rgb2oklab(hex2rgb(a)); y=rgb2oklab(hex2rgb(b)); return math.dist(x,y)
33: BRANDS={'Meta/Astryx accent':'#0064E0','Meta 0082FB':'#0082FB','Astryx accent dark':'#2694FE','Astryx text-accent dark':'#3E9EFB','Astryx text-blue dark':'#AFD7FF','Astryx text-blue light':'#042F97','Facebook':'#1877F2','LinkedIn':'#0A66C2','X legacy':'#1DA1F2','Vercel':'#0070F3','Docker':'#2496ED','VS Code':'#007ACC','Zoom':'#0B5CFF','Intel':'#0071C5','Samsung':'#1428A0','Linear':'#5E6AD2','Discord':'#5865F2','Bluesky':'#1185FE','Codeberg':'#2185D0','GitHub dark link':'#2F81F7','GitHub light link':'#0969DA','Material blue 500':'#2196F3','Bootstrap':'#0D6EFD','Signal':'#3A76F0','IBM blue 60':'#0F62FE'}
34: TW={'sky-200':'#BAE6FD','sky-300':'#7DD3FC','sky-400':'#38BDF8','sky-500':'#0EA5E9','sky-600':'#0284C7','sky-700':'#0369A1','sky-800':'#075985','sky-900':'#0C4A6E','blue-200':'#BFDBFE','blue-300':'#93C5FD','blue-400':'#60A5FA','blue-500':'#3B82F6','blue-600':'#2563EB','blue-700':'#1D4ED8','blue-800':'#1E40AF','blue-900':'#1E3A8A','indigo-200':'#C7D2FE','indigo-300':'#A5B4FC','indigo-400':'#818CF8','indigo-500':'#6366F1','indigo-600':'#4F46E5','indigo-700':'#4338CA','indigo-800':'#3730A3','indigo-900':'#312E81','cyan-300':'#67E8F9','cyan-400':'#22D3EE'}
35: ALL={**BRANDS,**{'tailwind '+k:v for k,v in TW.items()}}
36: import json, re, sys
37: def load_tokens(path):
38:     src = open(path, encoding='utf-8').read()
39:     m = re.search(r"```json design-tokens\n(.*?)\n```", src, re.S)
40:     if not m: sys.exit('design-tokens block not found')
41:     return json.loads(m.group(1))
42: def resolve(col, name):
43:     node = col
44:     for part in name.split('.'):
45:         node = node[part]
46:     return node[:7]
47: def main():
48:     t = load_tokens(sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 'spec/astryx/DESIGN.md')
49:     col = t['color']; fail = 0
50:     print('== Distinctness (identity colors must be >= 0.050 from every reference, >= 0.10 from Meta)')
51:     meta = ['#0064E0', '#2694FE', '#0082FB', '#1877F2']
52:     for name in ['signal', 'signalDim', 'ink', 'field.ground']:
53:         hx = resolve(col, name)
54:         d, ref = min((de(hx, c), k) for k, c in ALL.items())
55:         dm = min(de(hx, c) for c in meta)
56:         ok = d >= 0.0495 and dm >= 0.10
57:         fail += not ok
58:         print(f"{'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'} {name:14s} {hx} nearest={ref} dE={d:.3f} dE_meta={dm:.3f}")
59:     print('== Contrast')
60:     for a, b, need in t['contrastPairs']:
61:         r = cr(resolve(col, a), resolve(col, b)); ok = r >= need; fail += not ok
62:         print(f"{'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'} {a:12s} on {b:13s} {r:5.2f} (need {need})")
63:     sys.exit(1 if fail else 0)
64: if __name__ == '__main__':
65:     main()
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/BUILD-ORDER.md">
  1: # BUILD-ORDER.md: how the swarm builds this
  2: 
  3: Version 1.0.0, 2026-10-30.
  4: 
  5: This file is the orchestration. It splits the build into task cards small enough for an agent with no other context, says which can run at the same time, and names the gate that must be green before the next wave starts. A coordinator (human or agent) hands out cards, checks gates, and updates `BUILD-STATE.md`.
  6: 
  7: ## 1. Rules every agent follows
  8: 
  9: 1. **Read only your card's inputs.** Every card lists the exact files to read. DESIGN.md and the relevant spec are always enough. If they are not, the spec has a bug: stop and report it, do not guess.
 10: 2. **Never invent a value.** Colors, sizes, durations, counts, camera poses come from `src/tokens.generated.ts` (generated from DESIGN.md). Never type a hex, a millisecond value or a geometry constant in `src/`. If a value is missing from DESIGN.md, stop and report.
 11: 3. **Never invent copy.** Strings come from `src/copy.ts` (generated from S09). If a string is missing, stop and report.
 12: 4. **Stay in your files.** Each card lists the files it owns. Do not edit files owned by another card. Shared files (`package.json`, `App.tsx`) are edited only by the card that owns them; others request changes in `BUILD-STATE.md`.
 13: 5. **Done means green.** A card is done when every test in its "Done when" list passes and its receipts are in `qa/`. Paste the test output into the card's entry in `BUILD-STATE.md`.
 14: 6. **Blocked means written down.** Write `BLOCKED: <question> (<spec section>)` under your card in `BUILD-STATE.md` and stop. Do not work around a spec.
 15: 7. **Commit per card** on the build branch with message `B<n>.<m>: <card title>`, plus the attribution lines your environment requires. Never merge, never deploy.
 16: 8. **Version stamps.** If a card changes a spec or DESIGN.md (only coordinator-approved), bump that file's version and add a changelog line with date and reason.
 17: 
 18: ## 2. The waves
 19: 
 20: ```
 21: Wave 0   B0 Foundation ─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 22:                                                                      │ G-BUILD
 23: Wave 1   B1 Theme      B2 Engine core      B3 Router + travel (stub) │
 24:             │              │                    │                    │ G-TOKENS, G-CONTRAST(palette), G-WORLD(test layouts), G-TRAVEL(stub)
 25: Wave 2   B4 Chrome     B5a Server layout   B5b LeafLink layout   B5c SideQuests layout   B5d Scatter layout
 26:             │              │                    │                    │                    │ G-CHROME, layout unit tests
 27: Wave 3   B6a Server scene   B6b LeafLink scene   B6c SideQuests scene   B6d 404 scene
 28:             │                    │                    │                    │ G-SCENES, G-HUE, G-COPY
 29: Wave 4   B7 Fallbacks and posters
 30:             │ G-FALLBACK
 31: Wave 5   B8 Integration, perf lab, anti and content gates
 32:             │ G-PERF-LAB, G-ANTI, G-CONTENT, full G-* rerun
 33: Wave 6   B9 Device run (Justin) and handoff
 34:               G-PERF-DEVICE, ship rule (S11 section 9)
 35: ```
 36: 
 37: Maximum parallelism: 3 agents in wave 1, 5 in wave 2, 4 in wave 3. Waves 4 to 6 are single-agent.
 38: 
 39: ## 3. Task cards
 40: 
 41: Each card: inputs, owns, builds, done when.
 42: 
 43: ### B0 Foundation (wave 0, 1 agent)
 44: - **Inputs:** S00, DESIGN.md (whole), S09 section 4, repo `spec/standard-implementations.md`.
 45: - **Owns:** everything in S00 section 3 except `src/world/**`, `src/chrome/**`, `src/scenes/**`, `themes/lane/laneTheme.mjs`. Also creates `src/world/types.ts` with the S02 section 2 API types and `src/world/stub.ts`: a fake `createLanefield` that keeps weights and pose in memory, resolves `travelTo` after `T.motion.ms.travel` (or immediately when instant), and exposes the same `window.__lanefield` shape with `laneCount: 6144`. Creates `BUILD-STATE.md` with one section per card.
 46: - **Builds:** package pins, generators, lints, layer order, index.html, `src/lib/*` verbatim snippets, a minimal `App.tsx` that renders C-SRV-H1 on the void ground.
 47: - **Done when:** T-S00-1 to T-S00-6 pass. G-BUILD green.
 48: 
 49: ### B1 Theme (wave 1)
 50: - **Inputs:** S01, DESIGN.md sections 3 to 7 and 11, the `tokens.generated.json` from B0.
 51: - **Owns:** `themes/lane/**`, `src/styles/global.css` regime block, `qa/astryx/**`.
 52: - **Done when:** T-S01-1 to T-S01-5 (T-S01-5 can use a test page with one Button). G-TOKENS green for theme files.
 53: 
 54: ### B2 Engine core (wave 1)
 55: - **Inputs:** S02 (whole), DESIGN.md sections 6, 8, 12 `world`, `motion`, `budgets`.
 56: - **Owns:** `src/world/lanefield.ts`, `shaders/*`, `worker.ts` (with a pluggable generator registry), `camera.ts`, `tween.ts`, `governor.ts`, `debug.ts`, `tests/world.*.spec.ts`.
 57: - **Builds:** everything in S02 using four **test layouts** in place of real generators: server-test (6,144 straight lanes on a 96 by 64 grid), leaflink-test (6,144 random flat segments), sidequests-test (6,144 small circles' chords), scatter (implement the real S08 generator here; it is ten lines).
 58: - **Done when:** T-S02-1 to T-S02-8 pass on the test layouts; unit tests of section 7 numbers pass.
 59: 
 60: ### B3 Router and travel (wave 1)
 61: - **Inputs:** S03, S04 sections 3 and 8, S09 titles, DESIGN `chrome.travelDom`, `motion`.
 62: - **Owns:** `src/router.ts`, `src/travel.ts`, `tools/prerender.mjs`, `tests/travel.*.spec.ts`.
 63: - **Builds:** against `src/world/stub.ts`. Readouts are placeholder components that render the right H1 from copy.
 64: - **Done when:** T-S03-1 to T-S03-8 pass against the stub (T-S03-6 measures DOM feedback only in this wave).
 65: 
 66: **Gate after wave 1:** G-TOKENS, palette receipt, G-WORLD (test layouts), G-TRAVEL (stub). Then the coordinator swaps the stub for the real engine in `main.tsx` (one line) and reruns G-TRAVEL.
 67: 
 68: ### B4 Chrome (wave 2)
 69: - **Inputs:** S04, S01 section 5, S09, DESIGN sections 4, 5, 7, `chrome`.
 70: - **Owns:** `src/chrome/**`, `src/App.tsx`, `tests/chrome.*.spec.ts`.
 71: - **Done when:** T-S04-1 to T-S04-7 pass; axe zero on all routes with placeholder readouts.
 72: 
 73: ### B5a to B5d Layout generators (wave 2, 4 agents in parallel)
 74: - **Inputs:** S02 section 4.4 (worker contract, RNG), and one of: S05 section 3.1 (B5a), S06 section 3.1 (B5b), S07 section 3.1 (B5c), S08 section 3 (B5d). DESIGN `world.<layout>`.
 75: - **Owns:** `src/world/layouts/<layout>.ts`, `tests/layouts/<layout>.test.ts`.
 76: - **Contract:** `export function generate(seed: number, out: Float32Array, layoutIndex: number): SideData` writes 6,144 lanes by 8 points into `out` at the S02 texel indices and returns side data. Pure function, no DOM, no three.js imports.
 77: - **Done when:** lane total 6,144; determinism (hash equal across two runs); the scene's T-*-1 unit test passes (T-SRV-1, T-LL-1, T-SQ-1); generation under 150ms in Node on CI.
 78: 
 79: **Gate after wave 2:** G-CHROME, all layout unit tests. The coordinator registers the four real generators in the worker (replacing B2's test layouts) and reruns G-WORLD.
 80: 
 81: ### B6a to B6d Scenes (wave 3, 4 agents in parallel)
 82: - **Inputs:** the scene's spec (S05, S06, S07 or S08) whole; S02 sections 2, 5, 6, 8, 9 (API, shaders, clock, camera, picking); S04 section 6 (readout variants); DESIGN `world.<scene>`, `world.camera.<scene>`.
 83: - **Owns:** `src/world/<scene>.ts` (controller), `src/scenes/<scene>.tsx` (readout content), `tests/scenes/<scene>.spec.ts`.
 84: - **Done when:** every T-* in the scene spec passes; the scene's hue budget passes; G-COPY passes for the scene's route.
 85: 
 86: **Gate after wave 3:** G-SCENES, G-HUE, G-COPY on all routes.
 87: 
 88: ### B7 Fallbacks and posters (wave 4)
 89: - **Inputs:** S10, S03 section 7.
 90: - **Owns:** `tools/posters.mjs`, `public/posters/**`, noscript blocks, mode detection in `main.tsx`, `tests/fallback.*.spec.ts`.
 91: - **Done when:** T-S10-1 to T-S10-6. G-FALLBACK green.
 92: 
 93: ### B8 Integration and lab gates (wave 5)
 94: - **Inputs:** S11 whole; QA receipts so far.
 95: - **Owns:** `tools/qa-report.mjs`, `tools/trace-content.mjs`, `qa/content/facts.json`, `qa/anti/**`, Lighthouse config.
 96: - **Builds:** reruns every gate from a clean clone; writes `qa/QA-REPORT.md`; runs G-ANTI with a fresh reviewer agent that has read nothing but the screenshots and the questions; runs G-CONTENT.
 97: - **Done when:** every automated gate green, the kill list empty, `QA-REPORT.md` committed.
 98: 
 99: ### B9 Device run and handoff (wave 6)
100: - **Who:** Justin (or whoever deploys a preview; Claude never deploys).
101: - **Does:** S11 section 5 on the reference phone, attaches the JSON, resolves or accepts each UNKNOWN in UNKNOWNS.md.
102: - **Done when:** S11 section 9 ship rule holds. The build agent posts the commit SHA, `dist/` byte counts and the QA report link.
103: 
104: ## 4. BUILD-STATE.md format (created by B0)
105: 
106: ```
107: ## B2 Engine core
108: status: todo | doing | blocked | done
109: agent: <name>
110: commit: <sha>
111: tests: <paste of passing output, or failing test ids>
112: receipts: qa/world/...
113: notes: <one line per decision taken inside the spec's latitude>
114: BLOCKED: <question> (<spec section>)   # only when blocked
115: ```
116: 
117: ## 5. Critical path and risk
118: 
119: The critical path is B0, B2, B5 (slowest generator, likely SideQuests), B6c, B8, B9. The highest risks, in order: (1) real-phone frame time on the Server scene (6,144 thin lanes with per-fragment pulses), retired early by running G-PERF-DEVICE on B2's test layouts as soon as wave 1 lands, using a preview deploy; (2) the field theme override losing to Astryx's scoped CSS (S01 section 6), retired in B1; (3) troika text on the WebGL path compiling mid-travel, retired by S02 section 6 precompile and T-S02-6.
120: 
121: ## 6. Changelog
122: 
123: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/README.md">
 1: # Astryx Keystone: spec package for Justin Poli's personal site
 2: 
 3: Version 1.0.0, 2026-10-30. Written to be built by agents that have not seen the conversation that produced it.
 4: 
 5: ## The site in five lines
 6: 
 7: One WebGL field of 6,144 lanes, one per CUDA core on the RTX 3070 Ti in Justin's home inference server. On the home scene they run parallel and fire 32 at a time, converging to an output point where the stack's words come out and cool. Travel to LeafLink and the same lanes fall flat into ink streets on paper where requests and offers land and get routed to each other. Travel to SideQuests and they loosen into a chalk campus plan on blueprint blue with three quest trails you can walk. The only navigation is a terminal keymap on the bottom edge; nothing reloads.
 8: 
 9: ## Reading order
10: 
11: 1. `DESIGN.md`: every value. The `design-tokens` JSON block is authoritative and machine-read.
12: 2. `BUILD-ORDER.md`: waves, task cards, gates. Agents start here after DESIGN.md.
13: 3. `specs/`: one file per system or scene.
14:    - `S00-foundation.md` repo, pins, generators, lints
15:    - `S01-theme.md` the Astryx "lane" theme
16:    - `S02-lanefield-engine.md` renderer, data layout, GLSL, clock, camera, governor
17:    - `S03-travel-routing.md` routes, travel choreography, prerender
18:    - `S04-chrome.md` keymap, readouts, sheets
19:    - `S05-scene-server.md` home: the inference server
20:    - `S06-scene-leaflink.md` LeafLink: mutual aid map
21:    - `S07-scene-sidequests.md` SideQuests: campus plan and trails
22:    - `S08-scene-notfound.md` 404
23:    - `S09-copy-deck.md` every visible string, with sources
24:    - `S10-fallbacks-a11y.md` no-JS, no-WebGL, reduced motion, screen readers, posters
25:    - `S11-performance-qa.md` gates, budgets, kill list, ship rule
26: 4. `UNKNOWNS.md`: open questions and Justin's decisions.
27: 5. `TASTE-GATES.md`: the anti-slop, banned-style, anti-template and content-boundary runs on this package.
28: 6. `tools/palette-receipt.py`: rerunnable color receipts (`python3 -I spec/astryx/tools/palette-receipt.py spec/astryx/DESIGN.md`).
29: 
30: ## Sources this package is built from
31: 
32: `baseline/` (the 2026-10-30 package: theme, motion, ui-ux, material, design-extracts, 3d, content, process) and the live Astryx CLI 0.7.1 (`npx @astryxdesign/cli@0.7.1`), probed on 2026-10-30. The content brief is the factual boundary; S09 traces every string to it.
33: 
34: ## Standing rules
35: 
36: Claude never deploys or merges. No em or en dashes in site copy. No value outside DESIGN.md. No string outside S09. The PCB direction and the cipher/ember theme are dead and must not return.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/TASTE-GATES.md">
  1: # TASTE-GATES.md: the taste gates, run on this package
  2: 
  3: Version 1.0.1, 2026-10-30. Sources: `baseline/ui-ux/anti-slop-rubric.md`, `baseline/ui-ux/banned-slop-styles.md`, `baseline/ui-ux/ui-slop-patterns.md` section 6, `baseline/process/SPEC-DRIVEN-METHOD.md` section 5, `baseline/content/CONTENT-BRIEF.md`.
  4: 
  5: This file records what the gates found on the specs. It does not replace the gates on the built site: G-ANTI and G-CONTENT (S11 sections 6 and 7) rerun them on screenshots and on `src/copy.ts`.
  6: 
  7: ## 1. Anti-template test
  8: 
  9: The question: can someone name one concrete way this does not look generated?
 10: 
 11: The answer the package is built to produce: "The navigation is a one-line terminal keymap, and every scene is the same 6,144 GPU lanes rearranged." Both halves are mechanical, so the build can check them without taste: `laneCount === 6144` on every route, one draw call for the lanes, zero card classes, no `<header>` navbar, document height equals viewport height (DESIGN 2, S11 G-ANTI).
 12: 
 13: Verdict: PASS on the spec. It becomes PASS on the site only when a reviewer who has read nothing but the screenshots says something that specific (S11 G-ANTI question 1).
 14: 
 15: ## 2. Banned styles, run over every string in S09
 16: 
 17: Scope: 56 copy IDs, 86 backticked strings.
 18: 
 19: | Check | Result |
 20: |---|---|
 21: | Em or en dash, anywhere in the package (all `.md` and `tools/`) | 0 |
 22: | Lexical bans (section 1 of the ban list plus Justin's: passionate, leverage, cutting-edge, seamless, delve, tapestry, ninja, rockstar; plus signature, iconic, actually, simply, just, alone, honest, watch, notice) | 0 |
 23: | GPA, `2.77`, phone digits | 0 |
 24: | "live" | 2 allowed, both quoting the brief's description of LeafLink: "on a live map" (C-LL-P1) and "WebSocket live feed" (C-LL-STACK). S00 `banned.txt` allows exactly these two substrings and nothing else. |
 25: | Contrast frames ("not X, it's Y") | 0 |
 26: | Rule of three | **1 found, fixed.** C-LL-P1 read "Someone needs something, someone nearby has it, and the map connects them as it happens": three beats in the same cadence. Now "When a neighbor needs what you have, the map connects you as it happens." (S09 changelog) |
 27: | Aphorism closers, organic metaphors (breathes, lives), inserted jokes, stage-managing ("watch") | 0 |
 28: | Eyebrows, serial numbers, stat cards | 0. The design has no slot for any of them (S04 readout anatomy is heading plus prose plus one link). |
 29: 
 30: The feel-words from the launch prompt and the scene keywords in the brief ("heartbeat", "adventure", "gathering", "real-time pulse") are used in the specs to steer motion and are kept out of the copy on purpose. The page says what each project is; the motion carries the feeling.
 31: 
 32: ## 3. UI slop checklist (ui-slop-patterns section 6), predicted from the specs
 33: 
 34: | Line | Verdict | Where it is enforced |
 35: |---|---|---|
 36: | No purple to cyan gradient | CLEAN. No gradients at all; one hue. | DESIGN 3, 10 |
 37: | No glow without a light source | CLEAN. Brightness comes from lane overlap and pulses that travel on lanes; no bloom pass, no shadows (S00 lint bans non-inset `box-shadow`; S01 theme). | S02 renderer, S01 |
 38: | No nested cards | CLEAN. Zero cards; G-ANTI greps for them. | DESIGN 2 |
 39: | Every icon labels something | CLEAN. No icons. Keys are letters in `Kbd`. | S04 |
 40: | Every graph has a labeled denominator | CLEAN. No graphs, no numbers on screen (K-SRV-1). | S05 |
 41: | One typeface, on purpose | CLEAN. Commit Mono only, three weights, chosen because the navigation is a terminal. | DESIGN 4 |
 42: | No all-caps eyebrow | CLEAN. No uppercase transforms anywhere. | DESIGN 4, 10 |
 43: | No button wraps its label | CLEAN by test: T-S04-1 asserts the keymap never wraps at 320px. | S04 |
 44: | One radius system | CLEAN. Astryx radius scale only; Kbd uses `--radius-inner`. | DESIGN 5, S01 |
 45: | Squint test | Unverified until screenshots exist. On the renders made for S05, the readout column and the field separate clearly at blur. The contact action is one key (`c`) from every scene. | S11 G-ANTI |
 46: 
 47: ## 4. Anti-slop rubric, run over this package as model output
 48: 
 49: | Dimension | Verdict | Evidence |
 50: |---|---|---|
 51: | 1. Sycophancy | CLEAN | The package overrules the launch prompt's framing where evidence demanded it (section 6 below) and records its own wrong guesses (DESIGN changelog: the first lane alpha and the 12% hue budget, both disproved by renders). |
 52: | 2. Verdict evasion | CLEAN | Each decision has a default applied and a one-line reversal (UNKNOWNS section 1). No "it depends". |
 53: | 3. Scaffold as substance | FLAG, accepted | Every spec has the same section shape. That is the SPEC-DRIVEN-METHOD contract, which exists so agents can find things. The content inside each section is not templated: S05 is a formula and a camera, S06 is a simulation, S07 is a plan generator. |
 54: | 4. Verbosity as coverage | CLEAN with one risk | Values live once, in the DESIGN token block. Specs restate a few numbers only inside formulas so a reviewer can check them by hand (DESIGN 12 says code never reads the spec text). The risk is drift between the restatements and the tokens; `lint-tokens` (S00) and the scene unit tests are the guard. |
 55: | 5. Superlative substitution | CLEAN | No adjective carries a claim. "Bold" in the prompt became a mechanism (one lane field, three regimes), not a word in the copy. |
 56: | 6. Entrenchment | CLEAN | First server render saturated into a solid block; the parameters were changed and the change written down rather than defended. Counter-cases are in section 7. |
 57: | 7. Framing parroting | CLEAN | See section 6: the prompt said "seamless travel"; the package does not fly a camera between three places, it re-forms one object. |
 58: | 8. Citation theater | **FLAG, accepted by Justin** | C-SRV-ALT and the whole geometry rest on the RTX 3070 Ti having 48 SMs of 128 CUDA cores (6,144). That is NVIDIA's public GA104 spec, but no source was opened while writing this package. It is registered as R-GPU; Justin accepted it on 2026-10-30 without a check, which the ship rule allows. Every other factual string traces to a brief line (section 5). |
 59: | 9. Hedge accounting | CLEAN | Every UNKNOWN names the test or the person that resolves it (UNKNOWNS sections 2 to 4). |
 60: | 10. Legacy protectiveness | CLEAN | PCB board, cipher/ember theme, GSAP and the single-file constraint of the earlier site are all dead here and named dead (README standing rules). Nothing from v1 to v6 was carried forward for sunk cost. |
 61: 
 62: ## 5. Content boundary trace
 63: 
 64: Every BRIEF or MEM string in S09, against the line of `CONTENT-BRIEF.md` it rests on.
