  1: # Portfolio Spec: Justin Poli — "The Engineer Who Verifies"
  2: Status: DRAFT (pending design-extract tokens, then Claude review R1)
  3: Date: 2026-10-04
  4: Owner: Justin Poli (justinf0829@gmail.com, (631) 835-5490, Smithtown, NY)
  5: 
  6: ## 0. Judgment calls (flagged, not smoothed)
  7: 1. Reference sites for design-extract were chosen by Cipher, not Justin: bruno-simon.com and corentinbernadou.com (both Awwwards SOTD, portfolio genre, three.js + GSAP). A third candidate (Hiroto Sato) was dropped after its domain resolved to a ceramic artist. Justin or Claude may veto or swap these; the design token section is swappable without touching IA or features.
  8: 2. Single self-contained HTML file (inline CSS/JS, embedded font subset, CDN-pinned three.js r186 + GSAP via importmap). Rationale: byte-verifiable deploy, zero build step, matches the proven vercel-demo-launch pipeline. Trade-off: no framework; all component logic is hand-rolled and must pass the standard-implementations reference.
  9: 3. Project roster stays locked to LeafLink + SideQuests per GOAL.md. A third "lab shelf" slot (e.g., playable sudoku lie detector) is specced as OPTIONAL and clearly marked; adding it needs Justin's explicit call.
 10: 4. Contact form ships with a pluggable endpoint adapter and a mailto + copy-email fallback. No backend is wired at launch; wiring needs Justin's explicit approval (Day-1 trust boundary: no outbound without absolute approval).
 11: 5. Codeberg (https://codeberg.org/bon3head) is the canonical code URL on the site, per his 2026-10-02 recruiting preference. GitHub bon3head is linked secondarily. Live repo stats come from the GitHub public API (Codeberg's anti-AI tooling blocks programmatic fetch); stats are fail-soft and labeled with their source.
 12: 
 13: ## 1. Goal and audience
 14: Primary audience: technical hiring managers and senior engineers evaluating a new-grad SWE (backend/systems leaning, graduating Dec 2027) for full-time roles starting 2027.
 15: Audited finding (locked): motion is near-zero hiring signal for backend/full-stack SWE roles. Therefore: evidence is the payload, motion is the delivery vehicle. Every visual decision must serve evidence legibility. Motion that does not serve evidence is cut.
 16: 
 17: ## 2. Locked decisions (from GOAL.md and memory, not re-debatable here)
 18: - Direction: "the engineer who verifies." Every project dossier leads with failure class, receipt, and falsifiability test.
 19: - Roster: LeafLink, SideQuests. Both shipped (hackathon). No invented product claims anywhere: CanonClaw/ASH/Nexus are systems fluency, not products; Oracle/GRPO are not resume-ready.
 20: - Stack: three.js r186, WebGPU feature detection with WebGL2 fallback, TSL materials/shaders, GSAP choreography, postprocessing. No framework.
 21: - Voice: terse, technically dense. No em dashes in people-facing copy, ever. Banned-slop-styles gate on all copy.
 22: - Motion: scroll-scrubbed cinematic is the standing preference; motion-heavy welcome allowed only if it never blocks content.
 23: 
 24: ## 3. Anti-template constraint (named pre-build, per standing rule)
 25: The site must NOT:
 26: (a) open with a centered name + title + two CTA buttons hero;
 27: (b) use a project card grid with stock-style thumbnails;
 28: (c) use skill bar charts, percentage dials, or "services I offer" grids.
 29: Instead: the hero is a systems-diagram scene (the verification rig, §9) with evidence-first copy, and projects are full dossiers, not cards. Any build violating (a)-(c) fails QA regardless of polish.
 30: 
 31: ## 4. Information architecture
 32: Single-page application with view routing (vanilla JS, PJAX-style transitions, no framework). Views:
 33: - `/` Home: hero scene, proof strip, dossier index (2 dossiers, full-bleed rows, not cards), timeline, contact band.
 34: - `/about`: now/then, colophon (stack, pipeline, verification method), principles (DVD: data, verification, determinism).
 35: - `/work/leaflink`: dossier.
 36: - `/work/sidequests`: dossier.
 37: - `/lab` (OPTIONAL, flagged): experiments shelf.
 38: - `/contact`: contact view (also reachable as footer band on home).
 39: - 404 view with terminal-styled recovery.
 40: Deep-linkable views; back/forward button support; each view has a unique title and meta description.
 41: 
 42: ## 5. Page-by-page spec
 43: 
 44: ### 5.1 Home
 45: Sections in order:
 46: 1. Hero: full-viewport 3D verification-rig scene (§9), scroll-scrubbed assembly. Copy block (left-aligned, never centered): name, one-line thesis ("I build systems and prove they work." — copy deck to finalize; terse, no em dashes), two evidence links (dossiers). Scroll cue. All copy readable with WebGL disabled (scene is progressive enhancement; copy is DOM).
 47: 2. Proof strip: 3-4 receipted claims (e.g., "Shipped 2 hackathon projects, both placed 2nd" with links to repos/demos). Each claim carries its receipt inline. No unreceipted adjectives.
 48: 3. Dossier index: two full-bleed dossier rows. Each row: project name, one-line what-it-is, failure class it survived, receipt link, "read the dossier" link. Rows alternate media/text alignment (anti-template).
 49: 4. Timeline: education, roles (Math Club VP, Eleet Coders council, CS Teams treasurer), hackathons, job-hunt filings (no confidential details). Scroll-scrubbed progress line.
 50: 5. Contact band: email CTA, Codeberg/GitHub/LinkedIn links, copy-email button.
 51: 
 52: ### 5.2 About
 53: - Now/then: current status (CS senior, Dec 2027; job hunt), background (Smithtown, NY; SUNY New Paltz).
 54: - Colophon: exact stack with pinned versions, the verification pipeline (deterministic build, byte-verify, receipt chain), "how this site was built" with link to the spec's public summary if Justin approves.
 55: - Principles: DVD (data, verification, determinism) in his words, terse.
 56: 
 57: ### 5.3 Dossier template (applies to LeafLink and SideQuests)
 58: Fixed structure, no deviations:
 59: 1. Header: name, one-line what-it-is, ship date, team, role, repo + live demo links.
 60: 2. Failure class: what category of failure this project survived (e.g., "demo-day live demo risk," "scope collapse under timebox").
 61: 3. Receipt: verifiable artifacts (repo link, commit, demo recording, placement proof). Every claim hyperlinked.
 62: 4. Falsifiability test: "what would prove this project is weaker than claimed" — stated plainly.
 63: 5. Build notes: stack, hardest technical decision, what was cut and why.
 64: 6. Evidence gallery: screenshots with captions (lightbox modal, §6).
 65: No testimonials, no skill tags cloud, no "key features" marketing list.
 66: 
 67: ### 5.4 Contact
 68: - Email CTA (justinf0829@gmail.com), copy-email button with confirmation state.
 69: - Form: name, email, message. Client validation per standard-implementations (pragmatic email regex). Submit → endpoint adapter: tries configured endpoint; on no endpoint, falls back to mailto compose with prefilled body. Never silently drops a message: every submit path ends in a user-visible confirmation or an explicit fallback.
 70: - Links: Codeberg (primary), GitHub, LinkedIn, phone.
 71: 
 72: ### 5.5 404
 73: Terminal-styled: `$ 404: route not found`, list of valid routes, link home. No cutesy illustration.
 74: 
 75: ## 6. Components
 76: - Nav: fixed, minimal. Left: wordmark "JP" or "justin poli" (copy deck). Right: Work, About, Contact, theme? (no theme toggle v1; cut). Mobile: hamburger → full overlay menu (accessible dialog pattern per code-examples.md §1).
 77: - Hero scene (§9).
 78: - Dossier row: full-bleed alternating layout.
 79: - Timeline: vertical line, scroll-scrubbed progress fill, entries fade/slide on enter.
 80: - Terminal (§7 novelty): interactive terminal section on home or about. Commands: help, whoami, projects, verify, contact, clear. Real implementation (not a fake typing animation): input parsing, command registry, history (up/down). Unknown command → helpful error listing commands (Postel: forgiving input, strict clear feedback).
 81: - Lightbox modal: evidence gallery images. Focus trap, Escape closes, focus returns to trigger, aria-modal. Per code-examples.md §1. No modal-on-load anywhere (anti-pattern).
 82: - Footer: sitemap, colophon line, "built and verified" note, copyright with year.
 83: - Copy-email button: clipboard write with fallback (execCommand), confirmation state ("copied"), revert after 2s.
 84: 
 85: ## 7. Novelty features (unique hero points, all real, no slop)
 86: 1. Verification-rig hero: the 3D scene is a stylized "rig" (node graph / pipeline diagram in 3D) that assembles on scroll: nodes = data, verification, determinism; edges light up as the dossier claims scroll into view. It is a diagram of his method, not decoration.
 87: 2. Interactive terminal (§6): a working terminal, not theater.
 88: 3. Dossier falsifiability sections: no other new-grad portfolio leads with "here is how you could prove me wrong." This is the differentiator; protect it in copy review.
 89: 4. Receipted proof strip: every claim hyperlinked to its artifact at first paint, not buried.
 90: (Anti-slop check: none of these are gradient blobs, particle fields for their own sake, or stock imagery. The 3D serves the thesis.)
 91: 
 92: ## 8. Motion design
 93: Choreography (GSAP + ScrollTrigger, pinned versions in §11):
 94: - Welcome: hero scene fades/scales in over 900ms; copy lines stagger up 24px, 60ms stagger. Total welcome under 1.4s. Skip intro button if load exceeds budget? No: keep it fast instead (perf budget §13).
 95: - Scroll: hero rig assembles scrubbed to scroll (scrub: 1). Timeline progress line scrubbed. Section headers: y+28px fade on enter (once, not scrubbed). Dossier rows: subtle parallax on media (±6% max; Vestibular-safe).
 96: - View transitions: 220ms fade + 12px y-shift on route change (PJAX-style). No full-page wipes.
 97: - Micro: buttons scale 0.97 on active; links underline-grow on hover (desktop only); copy-email morphs to confirmation.
 98: - Reduced motion: `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` disables scrub, parallax, stagger, and transitions; content appears statically. This is a hard gate, not a nice-to-have.
 99: - Performance: motion never blocks first paint of copy; all ScrollTriggers created after load; kill triggers on view change (no leaks).
100: 
101: ## 9. 3D scene spec (verification rig)
102: - Renderer: three.js r186. Feature-detect WebGPU (`navigator.gpu`); use WebGPURenderer when available, else WebGLRenderer (WebGL2). If neither, scene container hides and the DOM copy carries the hero (no-JS/no-WebGL fallback, §14).
103: - Scene: abstract node-graph "rig": 3 primary nodes (DATA, VERIFY, DETERMINE as labeled sprites/planes), edges as lines/tubes that draw on scroll progress, subtle TSL-driven emissive pulse on the active node. Postprocessing: bloom (threshold high, subtle) + vignette. No particle-field wallpaper; every element maps to the thesis.
104: - Camera: slow dolly tied to scroll (scrubbed), clamped; idle drift ±2 degrees when tab visible and user idle (disabled under reduced motion).
105: - Sizing: canvas fills hero viewport, devicePixelRatio clamped to 2, resize observer, pause rendering when hero off-screen (IntersectionObserver) or tab hidden.
106: - Upstream anchors (anti-hallucination): three.js r186 WebGPURenderer docs, TSL (Three Shader Language) node docs, GSAP ScrollTrigger docs. Exact API usage must cite these in code comments at first use.
107: 
108: ## 10. Functionality
109: - Router: hash-based or history-API routing within the single file. History API preferred (clean URLs) with server fallback: since deploy is a single static file, use hash routing OR history API with a 200.html/404.html rewrite... Vercel static upload serves one file; history-API deep links would 404 on refresh. Decision: hash-based routing (`#/work/leaflink`) for reliability on static hosting. Clean URLs are cut for robustness (Tesler: the system bears the complexity of static hosting; the user gets working deep links either way).
110: - Terminal: command registry object; commands: help, whoami, projects, verify, contact, clear, theme? (no). History via ArrowUp/Down. Unknown input → "unknown command: X. Try: help". Case-insensitive, trimmed (Postel).
111: - Lightbox: per §6; image preloading for gallery neighbors.
112: - Copy-email: per §6.
113: - Form: per §5.4; endpoint adapter pattern:
114: ```js
115: const CONTACT_ENDPOINT = null; // set when Justin approves a backend
116: async function submitContact(data) {
117:   if (CONTACT_ENDPOINT) { /* POST JSON, handle errors, confirm */ }
118:   else { location.href = 'mailto:...?subject=...&body=' + encodeURIComponent(...); }
119:   // every path ends in visible confirmation
120: }
121: ```
122: - GitHub stats (fail-soft): fetch `https://api.github.com/users/bon3head/repos` client-side, cache in memory only, render "N public repos" style counts; on failure, hide the stats block silently (no error UI for a non-critical enhancement). Label source: "via GitHub API".
123: - No cookies, no tracking, no analytics v1 (cut for privacy posture; Vercel analytics is Justin's call post-launch).
124: 
125: ## 11. Backend / API surface
126: v1 has no custom backend. The only network calls:
127: 1. CDN: three.js r186 + GSAP (pinned versions, importmap; SRI hashes where the CDN provides them).
128: 2. Fonts: embedded subset (no Google Fonts request at runtime; fonts inlined as woff2 data URIs).
129: 3. GitHub public API (fail-soft, §10).
130: 4. Contact endpoint: null at launch (mailto fallback).
131: Future (needs Justin approval): contact form endpoint (Vercel serverless or form service), view counter. Specced as adapters, not built.
132: 
133: ## 12. Design tokens (filled 2026-10-04 from design-extract v2: corentinbernadou.com + bruno-simon.com)
134: Full brief: `~/workspace/portfolio/design-extract/style-brief.md`.
135: - Canvas: warm near-black `#070304`; raised `#141414`; hairlines `#1f1f1f`.
136: - Ink: `#f5f5f5` primary, `#858585` secondary, `#575757` tertiary.
137: - Accent: UNLOCKED — reference ships brand red `#ff0000` (do not copy). Proposal: signal amber `#ffb000` (instrumentation connotation; verify AA). Claude/Justin to lock.
138: - Material: flat. No shadows, no gradients, no blur. Radius 3px max.
139: - Type: tight Title Case headings; narrow prose measure (~38-60ch); micro labels uppercase + letterspaced. Re-derive absolute scale at true 1x (extractor measured a scaled render).
140: - Spacing: 2px base; hairline rules separate sections, not cards; 12-column discipline.
141: - Motion: 300ms `cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94)`; scroll-linked hero/timeline.
142: - Do-not-copy: red brand color, issue-numbering system, grid-ruler tool, cursor-mask nav, logos, photography, proprietary fonts.
143: Anti-template re-check: these tokens produce editorial sections + full-bleed dossier rows; they do not produce (a) centered hero, (b) card grid, (c) skill bars (§3). Confirmed.
144: 
145: ## 13. Copy requirements (copy deck is a build input)
146: - Voice: terse, technically dense. Short lines. No em dashes, ever. No colons in headlines where avoidable is not required here (that rule is application prose); still prefer plain sentences.
147: - Every factual claim about Justin hyperlinked to its receipt on first appearance (repo, commit, demo, transcript, certificate).
148: - Banned: "passionate," "leverage," "cutting-edge," "seamless," "delve," "tapestry," "I am excited to announce" energy, superhero metaphors, "ninja/rockstar." All copy passes `~/workspace/distill/banned-slop-styles.md` before build.
149: - Numbers: dates, team sizes must match MEMORY.md exactly (Dec 2027, etc.). No rounding that changes meaning. GPA is CUT from the site entirely (Justin's call 2026-10-04: subpar, would need a defense).
150: - The copy deck (final strings for every section) must be locked before build; the builder does not invent copy.
151: 
152: ## 14. SEO, meta, performance budgets
153: - Title: "Justin Poli — Systems-minded SWE" (deck to finalize). Meta description one sentence, terse.
154: - OG tags with absolute URLs (set at deploy when the alias is known); favicon: inline SVG data URI (simple geometric mark, not emoji).
155: - Perf budgets: first contentful paint of DOM copy < 1.2s on broadband; total single-file size < 900KB excluding CDN libs; three.js scene init must not block copy paint (init after load event, async).
156: - Accessibility: semantic landmarks, skip link, focus-visible styles, all interactive targets ≥44px (§ui-ux-pro-max instincts), color contrast AA, keyboard-operable terminal and lightbox, `prefers-reduced-motion` honored.
157: 
158: ## 15. Edge states and fallbacks
159: - No JavaScript: all copy, nav (as anchor list), dossiers, and contact email link readable. 3D, terminal, and transitions absent gracefully.
160: - No WebGL / WebGPU: hero copy renders over a static CSS gradient-free backdrop (flat color from token system); scene container hidden.
161: - Reduced motion: §8.
162: - 393px width: single column; nav collapses to accessible overlay menu; hero copy stacks above a minimized scene or static backdrop; tap targets ≥44px.
163: - Offline after first load: n/a (no service worker v1; cut).
164: - CDN failure: if three.js fails to load, scene init is skipped silently and the static hero stands. The page never shows a broken canvas or an error to the visitor.
165: - GitHub API failure: stats block hidden (§10).
166: - Form with no endpoint: mailto fallback (§5.4, §10).
