<file path="spec/standard-implementations.md">
  1: # Standard implementations
  2: 
  3: Canonical implementations for things engineers hand-roll badly. Reach for these before writing your own. Each entry: the standard, the hand-rolled failure it replaces, and the smallest correct code. Links are the HD source.
  4: 
  5: ## Email validation
  6: - Standard: pragmatic client-side sanity check; the confirmation email is the real validation.
  7: ```js
  8: const EMAIL_RE = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
  9: if (!EMAIL_RE.test(value)) return 'Enter a valid email address.';
 10: ```
 11: - Bad: the multi-kilobyte RFC 5322 monster regex. It rejects valid addresses, accepts invalid ones, and nobody can audit it.
 12: - Server-side thoroughness: [isEmail.js](https://github.com/validatorjs/validator.js/blob/master/src/lib/isEmail.js) (validatorjs/validator.js)
 13: 
 14: ## URL validation
 15: - Standard: the platform parser. Never a regex.
 16: ```js
 17: function isHttpUrl(s) {
 18:   try {
 19:     const u = new URL(s);
 20:     return u.protocol === 'http:' || u.protocol === 'https:';
 21:   } catch { return false; }
 22: }
 23: ```
 24: - Bad: hand-rolled URL regexes. They miss IDNs, ports, auth segments, and unicode, and they rot.
 25: 
 26: ## UUIDs
 27: - Standard: `crypto.randomUUID()`, RFC 4122 v4, CSPRNG-backed, available in browsers and Node 19+.
 28: ```js
 29: const id = crypto.randomUUID(); // 'xxxxxxxx-xxxx-4xxx-yxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx'
 30: ```
 31: - Bad: `Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)` is not collision-safe, not crypto-safe, and has the wrong length distribution.
 32: 
 33: ## Debounce (trailing edge)
 34: - Standard:
 35: ```js
 36: function debounce(fn, ms) {
 37:   let t;
 38:   return (...args) => { clearTimeout(t); t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms); };
 39: }
 40: const onType = debounce(runSearch, 250);
 41: ```
 42: - Bad: firing the handler on every keystroke, or setTimeout soup with no cancel (stale requests race and overwrite fresh results).
 43: 
 44: ## HTML escaping (XSS)
 45: - Standard: escape the five characters, or skip strings entirely.
 46: ```js
 47: const ESC = { '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' };
 48: const escapeHtml = (s) => s.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => ESC[c]);
 49: // preferred: el.textContent = userInput (never innerHTML with user data)
 50: ```
 51: - Bad: `s.replace(/<script.*?>.*?<\/script>/gi, '')` is bypassable six ways (event handlers, svg, unicode, nesting). Blocklist-stripping HTML with regex is never correct.
 52: 
 53: ## Luhn check (card numbers)
 54: - Standard: ISO/IEC 7812 checksum. Catches typos and transpositions, not fraud.
 55: ```js
 56: function luhn(num) {
 57:   const ds = num.replace(/\D/g, '').split('').reverse().map(Number);
 58:   if (!ds.length) return false;
 59:   const sum = ds.reduce((a, d, i) => a + (i % 2 ? (d * 2 > 9 ? d * 2 - 9 : d * 2) : d), 0);
 60:   return sum % 10 === 0;
 61: }
 62: ```
 63: - Bad: length-only or prefix-only checks that accept `4111 1111 1111 1112`.
 64: 
 65: ## Password strength
 66: - Standard: [zxcvbn](https://github.com/dropbox/zxcvbn), which scores 0-4 from real crack-time estimates (dictionary, patterns, l33t, repeats).
 67: ```js
 68: const { score } = zxcvbn(password); // accept score >= 3
 69: ```
 70: - Bad: composition rules ("one uppercase, one number, one symbol"). NIST 800-63B retired them: they produce `P@ssw0rd1`, compliant and crackable in seconds.
 71: 
 72: ## Slugify
 73: - Standard:
 74: ```js
 75: const slugify = (s) => s.toLowerCase().normalize('NFD')
 76:   .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, '')   // strip diacritics: 'café' -> 'cafe'
 77:   .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
 78:   .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '');
 79: ```
 80: - Bad: `s.replace(/ /g, '-')` leaves uppercase, accents, and punctuation in URLs.
 81: 
 82: ## Throttle (leading + trailing)
 83: - Standard: for scroll/resize/pointer handlers. Debounce waits for quiet; throttle caps the rate.
 84: ```js
 85: function throttle(fn, ms) {
 86:   let last = 0, timer = null;
 87:   return (...args) => {
 88:     const now = Date.now();
 89:     const remaining = ms - (now - last);
 90:     if (remaining <= 0) {
 91:       if (timer) { clearTimeout(timer); timer = null; }
 92:       last = now;
 93:       fn(...args);
 94:     } else if (!timer) {
 95:       timer = setTimeout(() => { last = Date.now(); timer = null; fn(...args); }, remaining);
 96:     }
 97:   };
 98: }
 99: ```
100: - Bad: using debounce on scroll (fires only after scrolling stops, so scrub-linked UI lags) or no rate cap at all (layout thrash on every scroll event).
101: 
102: ## Copy to clipboard (with fallback)
103: - Standard: Clipboard API first, execCommand fallback, boolean result. The caller owns the confirmation UI.
104: ```js
105: async function copyText(text) {
106:   try {
107:     await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
108:     return true;
109:   } catch {
110:     // non-secure contexts, older browsers, denied permission
111:     const ta = document.createElement('textarea');
112:     ta.value = text;
113:     ta.style.position = 'fixed';
114:     ta.style.opacity = '0';
115:     document.body.appendChild(ta);
116:     ta.select();
117:     let ok = false;
118:     try { ok = document.execCommand('copy'); } catch {}
119:     ta.remove();
120:     return ok;
121:   }
122: }
123: // usage: const ok = await copyText(email);
124: // show 'copied' on true, 'copy failed, email shown' on false. Never silent.
125: ```
126: - Bad: `navigator.clipboard.writeText` with no catch (throws on http, in iframes, on denial — unhandled rejection, no feedback) or execCommand-only (dead on modern mobile Safari).
127: 
128: ## Reduced-motion listener
129: - Standard: matchMedia once, listen for changes, toggle a class the CSS and JS both read.
130: ```js
131: const motionMQ = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
132: function applyMotionPref() {
133:   document.documentElement.classList.toggle('reduced-motion', motionMQ.matches);
134:   // JS animation code reads the same class / motionMQ.matches before starting
135: }
136: motionMQ.addEventListener('change', applyMotionPref);
137: applyMotionPref();
138: ```
139: - Bad: checking `matchMedia(...).matches` once at load and never listening (misses the user toggling the OS setting mid-session), or sniffing UA strings.
140: 
141: ## Safe storage
142: - Standard: localStorage throws in private mode and under storage pressure. Never let it take the page down.
143: ```js
144: const safeStorage = {
145:   get(k) { try { return localStorage.getItem(k); } catch { return null; } },
146:   set(k, v) { try { localStorage.setItem(k, v); return true; } catch { return false; } },
147:   del(k) { try { localStorage.removeItem(k); } catch {} },
148: };
149: ```
150: - Bad: bare `localStorage.setItem` in a terminal-history or theme init path — one SecurityError in Safari private mode and the whole script dies.
151: 
152: ## Timestamps
153: - Standard: always UTC ISO 8601, always sortable.
154: ```js
155: new Date().toISOString(); // '2026-10-04T22:15:30.000Z'
156: ```
157: - Bad: hand-rolled date math across DST boundaries, locale-formatted strings stored as data. For arithmetic use [Temporal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-temporal) or date-fns. Never manual day/month rollover.
