<file path="spec/astryx/specs/S03-travel-routing.md">
  1: # S03 Travel and routing: one space, four addresses
  2: 
  3: Version 1.1.0, 2026-11-01. Depends on: S00, S02 (API only; S03 can be built against a stub), DESIGN.md section 6. Blocks: S04, S05 to S08, S10, S11.
  4: 
  5: ## 1. Purpose
  6: 
  7: The visitor moves between scenes without a reload. Each scene has a real URL (deep links, back and forward work, the page is shareable), but a URL change is a camera move and a lane re-formation inside the same world, never a navigation. This spec owns the router, the travel choreography between DOM and world, focus and announcements, and the prerendered HTML for every address.
  8: 
  9: ## 2. Routes
 10: 
 11: | Path | Scene | Layout | Regime | Title (S09) |
 12: |---|---|---|---|---|
 13: | `/` | Server | `server` | `void` | C-TITLE-SERVER |
 14: | `/leaflink` | LeafLink | `leaflink` | `paper` | C-TITLE-LEAFLINK |
 15: | `/sidequests` | SideQuests | `sidequests` | `field` | C-TITLE-SIDEQUESTS |
 16: | anything else | Not found | `scatter` | `void` | C-TITLE-404 |
 17: 
 18: Sheets are not routes. `#whoami` and `#contact` in the URL open the matching sheet over the current scene on load and are written to the URL when a sheet opens (`history.replaceState`, so closing a sheet does not cost a back press). Trailing slashes normalize to no slash. Uppercase normalizes to lowercase.
 19: 
 20: Scene order for adjacency keys: server (1), leaflink (2), sidequests (3). The 404 scene is outside the order.
 21: 
 22: ## 3. Router (`src/router.ts`)
 23: 
 24: - State: `{scene: SceneId, sheet: 'whoami' | 'contact' | null}`.
 25: - `go(scene)`: if `scene === current`, do nothing. Otherwise `history.pushState({scene}, '', path)`, then run travel (section 4).
 26: - `popstate`: read `location.pathname`, run travel to that scene without pushing.
 27: - Internal links are `<a href="/leaflink">` (real anchors, work with JS off). A click handler on the document intercepts same-origin clicks on anchors with `data-route`, unless a modifier key is held or the button is not the primary one, and calls `go`.
 28: - On first load the scene comes from `location.pathname`; the world starts directly in that layout (`createLanefield({initial})`), no intro travel.
 29: 
 30: ## 4. Travel choreography (the one transition)
 31: 
 32: Times are from the moment of input (`t = 0`). Durations come from DESIGN tokens.
 33: 
 34: | t (ms) | DOM | World |
 35: |---|---|---|
 36: | 0 | Keymap target item turns accent (`--duration-fast`, ease). Old readout starts fading: opacity 1 to 0, `--duration-fast`, ease. `aria-busy=true` on the readout region. | `travelTo(target)` starts: lane weights and camera pose tween over `--jp-duration-travel` (700) with `easeSignature`. Clear color tweens over 700 with `easePlain`. |
 37: | 150 | Old readout removed from the accessibility tree (`hidden`). | |
 38: | 350 | `<html data-regime>`, `color-scheme`, `<meta name=theme-color>` switch to the target regime. `document.title` updates. | Ground is halfway between regimes in luminance at this point (plain ease is symmetric near the middle), so the DOM text color flip lands where the old and new text colors are both low-contrast but no text is visible (readout hidden since 150). |
 39: | 400 | New readout enters: opacity 0 to 1 and translateY 8px to 0, `--duration-medium` (300), `easeSignature`. | |
 40: | 700 | | Travel resolves. Scene controller for the target starts its ambient loop; the previous controller has paused at t = 0. |
 41: | 700 | `aria-busy=false`. Focus moves to the new readout's heading (`tabindex=-1`, `focus({preventScroll: true})`) only if focus was inside the old readout or on the keymap. The `aria-live=polite` region announces the new H1 (C-SR-TRAVEL). | |
 42: 
 43: Rules:
 44: - **Interruptible.** A new `go()` during travel cancels the DOM timers, keeps the current lane weights and camera pose as the new start, and travels to the new target with a fresh 700ms. Weights stay normalized. No queueing, no waiting.
 45: - **Morph snapshot and retarget.** The morph controller (W5, DESIGN.md section 6.7) holds snapshot, target, and start time. `navigate(to)` takes a snapshot of the current interpolated values (scene weights, gradient parameters, ring center and radius, silhouette strength, logo opacity and scale, signature amplitudes), sets the target and start time, and pushes history immediately, so back works mid-flight. The previous morph is discarded, never queued. No per-lane start-time stagger: all lanes start at t = 0 and vary only in path, never in start time.
 46: - **Morph phases.** Release (0.00 to 0.30), Gather (0.15 to 0.75), Reveal (0.55 to 1.00), per DESIGN.md section 6.7 and tokens `motion.morph` and `morph.*`. The logo looms in during Reveal (scale `morph.loomFrom` to 1.00); on exit it recedes (scale 1.00 to `morph.recedeTo`, opacity to 0, window 0 to 0.40) and the ring collapses (0.20 to 0.80). Project to project shrinks the ring to `morph.ringBreath` 0.6r and regrows it to r, traveling between anchors on `curve.track` if the anchors differ.
 47: - **Direction from keymap order.** Direction follows keymap order 1, 2, 3, W: server (1), leaflink (2), sidequests (3), whoami (W, a sheet over the current scene, not a route). History back and forward use the same morph path, with direction taken from that order: moving toward a higher-order scene runs the forward morph, moving toward a lower-order scene runs the reverse morph. The 404 scene is outside the order; entering or leaving it uses the same morph grammar with the scatter layout as its endpoint.
 48: - **Focus at Reveal start.** The section 4 table row that moves focus at 700ms is superseded by the morph rule: focus moves to the new readout's heading at Reveal start (0.55 of travel), under the existing condition (only if focus was inside the old readout or on the keymap), and never while the Contact sheet focus lock is active. The incoming title arrives at Reveal start; outgoing title and copy exit over 150ms at t = 0.
 49: - **Degrade path.** If p95 frame time during a morph exceeds budget for `perf.degradeFrames` 30 consecutive frames, silhouette pull drops to 0 for the rest of the session. Lanes, gradient, and ring stay. This is logged to the dev HUD.
 50: - **One dominant element.** Nothing else animates between 150 and 400 except the world.
 51: - **Input never blocked.** Keys and taps work during travel.
 52: - **Reduced motion.** World cuts instantly (`travelTo(target, {instant: true})`). DOM: old readout fades out over `--duration-fast`, regime switches at 150, new readout fades in over `--duration-fast` with no translate. Total 300ms, all opacity.
 53: - **No world (WebGL2 missing).** The poster image crossfades with the readout using the reduced-motion timing, whether or not reduced motion is on.
 54: 
 55: ## 5. Scene controllers
 56: 
 57: Each scene module in `src/world/` exports `{id, start(lf: Lanefield), pause(), resume(), reduced(on: boolean)}`. The travel module calls `pause()` on the outgoing controller at t = 0 and `resume()` on the incoming one at t = 700 (or immediately on instant travel). Controllers must be idempotent and must not allocate per frame.
 58: 
 59: ## 6. Keyboard
 60: 
 61: Bindings registered once with Astryx `useHotkeys` (S04 owns the visual Keymap):
 62: 
 63: | Key | Action |
 64: |---|---|
 65: | `1` `2` `3` | `go('server' / 'leaflink' / 'sidequests')` |
 66: | `ArrowLeft` / `ArrowRight` | previous / next in scene order, wrapping; from 404, `ArrowRight` goes to server |
 67: | `w` | toggle WhoamiSheet |
 68: | `c` | toggle ContactSheet |
 69: | `Escape` | close the open sheet (BottomSheet handles this itself; do not double-handle) |
 70: 
 71: Bindings are disabled while a sheet is open, except `Escape` and the sheet's own key (`isDisabled` on the entries). They are never active inside inputs (there are none, but the Astryx default protects future ones).
 72: 
 73: ## 7. Prerender (`tools/prerender.mjs`)
 74: 
 75: After `vite build`, render the React app to string for each of `/`, `/leaflink`, `/sidequests` and the 404 with `react-dom/server` `renderToString`, inject into `dist/index.html`'s `#root`, set `<title>`, `<meta name=description>`, `<html data-regime>` and `style="color-scheme"`, and write:
 76: 
 77: - `dist/index.html` (server), `dist/leaflink/index.html`, `dist/sidequests/index.html`, `dist/404.html`.
 78: 
 79: The client hydrates with `hydrateRoot`. Hydration must not change any text (React reports mismatches in dev; T-S03-5 fails on any `console.error` during hydration). Static hosting serves `404.html` for unknown paths with status 404. Hosting is configured by the deployer (Claude never deploys); the build only emits these files.
 80: 
 81: ## 8. Kill criteria
 82: 
 83: - K-S03-1: any route change causes a document load (Playwright 1.64 fires `framenavigated` for `history.pushState` too, so that event cannot detect a load. Detect a load instead by a second `load` event firing, or by a window marker set before travel not surviving it).
 84: - K-S03-2: back or forward leaves the URL and the scene out of sync.
 85: - K-S03-3: travel ever waits for something (a fetch, a shader compile, a font) before the lanes start moving.
 86: - K-S03-4: text visible on screen at less than its contrast requirement at any sampled frame of a travel (G-TRAVEL-CONTRAST: Playwright samples screenshots every 50ms through a server-to-leaflink travel and checks that any pixel region with readable text has its required contrast against the pixels behind it; the readout is hidden between 150 and 400ms, so this should be trivially true).
 87: - K-S03-5: a deep link to `/sidequests` shows any other scene's readout, even for one frame.
 88: 
 89: ## 9. Receipts
 90: 
 91: | Test | Claim | How |
 92: |---|---|---|
 93: | T-S03-1 | No reload | K-S03-1 check across 20 random `go` calls |
 94: | T-S03-2 | Deep links | load each of the four paths directly; readout H1, `data-regime`, `__lanefield.weights()` one-hot index, and `document.title` match section 2 |
 95: | T-S03-3 | History | `/` then `2` then `3` then back, back: URL `/`, scene server; forward: `/leaflink` |
 96: | T-S03-4 | Interruptible | press `2`, wait 200ms, press `3`; travel ends on sidequests by 900ms after the second press; weights normalized throughout |
 97: | T-S03-5 | Clean hydration | zero `console.error` on each prerendered path |
 98: | T-S03-6 | Feedback under 250ms | timestamp keydown to the first frame where keymap item color changed and `weights()` moved: under 250ms on the CI runner (long-task-free) |
 99: | T-S03-7 | Focus and announce | with focus on the keymap, press `2`; after 700ms `document.activeElement` is the LeafLink H1 and the live region text is `LeafLink` |
100: | T-S03-8 | Reduced motion timing | with `reducedMotion: 'reduce'`, travel completes (new readout fully opaque) within 320ms and `weights()` is one-hot within one frame |
101: 
102: ## 10. Unknowns
103: 
104: None.
105: 
106: ## 11. Changelog
107: 
108: - 1.1.0, 2026-11-01: W1 morph rules: snapshot and retarget semantics, Release/Gather/Reveal phases, keymap-order direction for back and forward, focus at Reveal start (superseding the 700ms focus row), degrade path. No route, key, or copy change.
109: - 1.0.1, 2026-10-30: K-S03-1 detection corrected. Playwright 1.64 fires framenavigated for history.pushState, so that event false-fails correct code. Detection is now a second load event or a non-surviving window marker. Requirement unchanged: no route change causes a document load.
110: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/specs/S04-chrome.md">
  1: # S04 Chrome: Keymap, Readout, sheets, wordmark
  2: 
  3: Version 1.1.0, 2026-11-01. Depends on: S00, S01, S03, S09. Blocks: S05 to S08, S10, S11.
  4: 
  5: ## 1. Purpose
  6: 
  7: The DOM layer that persists across scenes. It is deliberately small: one terminal keymap line at the bottom edge (the only navigation), one readout per scene, two sheets that rise from the keymap like TUI panes, a toast, a wordmark. It carries all text, all links, all keyboard paths. The world behind it is decoration only for assistive tech (`aria-hidden`), so the chrome must be complete on its own.
  8: 
  9: ## 2. Visual identity
 10: 
 11: The chrome is a terminal UI in Commit Mono set on the scene's ground, flat, no containers except where a scene's readout variant calls for one. It changes color with the regime automatically through Astryx `light-dark()` tokens and the field override (S01 section 6). It never changes shape between scenes; only the readout's variant does.
 12: 
 13: ## 3. Page structure (exact landmarks)
 14: 
 15: ```html
 16: <body data-astryx-theme="lane">
 17:   <div id="world" aria-hidden="true"><!-- canvas, poster img --></div>
 18:   <div id="root">
 19:     <a class="jp-wordmark" href="/" data-route>Justin Poli</a>      <!-- hidden on Server -->
 20:     <main id="scene" aria-busy="false">
 21:       <section class="jp-readout jp-readout--server" aria-labelledby="h-server"> ... </section>
 22:       <div role="img" aria-label="C-SRV-ALT text"></div>            <!-- describes the world; S05 to S08 set it -->
 23:     </main>
 24:     <nav class="jp-keymap" aria-label="Scenes and panels"> ... </nav>
 25:     <div class="jp-live" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></div> <!-- visually hidden -->
 26:     <!-- BottomSheets portal here (Astryx LayerProvider) -->
 27:   </div>
 28: </body>
 29: ```
 30: 
 31: One `<h1>` per scene: the readout heading. No `<header>`, no `<footer>`.
 32: 
 33: ## 4. Keymap (`src/chrome/Keymap.tsx`)
 34: 
 35: **Anatomy:** a `<nav>` fixed to the bottom edge, full width, height `--jp-keymap-h` (36px fine, 44px coarse), padding-bottom `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`, background `--jp-ground` (opaque, so it reads over any lane), a 1px top hairline ring. Inside, one row of five items, left-aligned with `--jp-gutter` inset, gap `--spacing-6` (fine) or evenly distributed (coarse).
 36: 
 37: Each item is a real link or button:
 38: - Scenes: `<a href="/" data-route aria-current="page"?>`, `<a href="/leaflink" ...>`, `<a href="/sidequests" ...>`.
 39: - Panels: `<button aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded>` for whoami and contact.
 40: - Content: on fine pointers `<Kbd keys="1" />` followed by a space and the label `server` (S09 C-KEY-*). On coarse pointers the Kbd is hidden (`display: none`, not just transparent) and the label is the full target (44px tall, at least 44px wide).
 41: 
 42: **States** (color shift only, `--duration-fast`, `--ease-standard`):
 43: | State | Label color | Kbd ring |
 44: |---|---|---|
 45: | idle | `--color-text-secondary` | `--color-border` |
 46: | hover / focus-visible | `--color-text-primary` | `--color-text-secondary` |
 47: | active scene (`aria-current=page`) or open panel (`aria-expanded=true`) | `--color-accent` | `--color-accent` |
 48: 
 49: **Rules:** never wraps (width test at 320px CSS: all five items fit on one line; if not, reduce gap to `--spacing-3` under 360px, never shrink text below 13px, never truncate). No icons. No hamburger. No transform on hover.
 50: 
 51: ## 5. Wordmark (`src/chrome/Wordmark.tsx`)
 52: 
 53: `Justin Poli` (C-WORDMARK) as a link to `/`, top-left at `--jp-gutter`, `--text-label`, weight 700, color `--color-text-primary`. Hidden on Server (the H1 is the name there) with `hidden`, not opacity. Fades with the readout during travel.
 54: 
 55: **Name clear zone (W1).** The wordmark bounding box expanded by `zone.name.padPx` (12px) on every side is reserved: no scene title, readout, hero, or keymap may intersect it (DESIGN.md section 5). W0 measured the coarse name box as left 16, top 16, right 126, bottom 40, so the coarse clear box is left 4, top 4, right 138, bottom 52. Scene titles size to fit: a title renders at the smaller of its token size and the available width divided by characters times the Commit Mono advance. T-NAME-1 checks the zone on every route, and T-NAME-2 checks it mid-morph.
 56: 
 57: ## 6. Readout (`src/chrome/Readout.tsx`)
 58: 
 59: One component, three variants, plus the 404 which reuses `server`. Content comes from S09 by ID; the variant decides placement and container. Max width `--jp-readout-max` (520px), prose max 52ch.
 60: 
 61: | Variant | Placement fine (width at least 1024) | Placement coarse or narrow | Container |
 62: |---|---|---|---|
 63: | `server` | Left column: `left: gutter`, vertically anchored so its bottom sits `3 * --spacing-12` above the keymap; width `chrome.serverReadoutWidth` (min(36vw, 480px)) | Top: `top: gutter + 8px`, full width minus gutters | None. Text sits on void ground. S05 guarantees the lanes never paint behind it (camera shift). |
 64: | `leaflink` | Bottom-left legend slip: `left: gutter`, `bottom: keymap + gutter`, width 360px | Bottom sheet-like slip above the keymap, full width minus gutters, max height 46dvh, scrolls internally | `--jp-raised` (paper.raised) fill, 0 radius, hairline ring. It is a map legend: it is allowed to cover map; the map is never behind its text. |
 65: | `sidequests` | Top-right trailhead: `right: gutter`, `top: gutter + 40px`, width 380px | Top, full width minus gutters | No fill. A 1px dashed chalk outline (`outline: 1px dashed var(--color-text-secondary); outline-offset: --spacing-3`), like a sign drawn on the plan. Text sits on field ground; S07 keeps everything except the 10% grid out from behind it with the camera shift (S07 section 3.2). |
 66: 
 67: Typography inside every variant: H1 `--jp-text-display-0` on server, `--jp-text-display-1` on leaflink and sidequests; paragraphs `--text-body`; facts and stack lines `--text-supporting` in `--color-text-secondary`; links `Link` (Astryx) with `isExternalLink`, color accent, underline on hover only. Spacing: H1 to first paragraph `--spacing-6`, paragraph to paragraph `--spacing-4`, prose to supporting lines `--spacing-6`, supporting lines `--spacing-2` apart, links row `--spacing-6` above, links `--spacing-6` apart.
 68: 
 69: Phones: the readout region may scroll (`overflow-y: auto`, `overscroll-behavior: contain`); the page itself never scrolls (`html, body { height: 100%; overflow: hidden; }`). Touch on the canvas region must not be captured by the readout. On coarse or narrow viewports the readout top sits below the name clear zone (section 5); no readout may intersect it on any viewport.
 70: 
 71: ## 7. Sheets
 72: 
 73: Both are Astryx `BottomSheet` with `purpose="info"`, `height="hug"`, `hasScrim` true, `label` from S09, controlled by router state. On fine pointers the sheet's inner content is constrained to 640px and left-aligned with the keymap gutter (a pane rising above the keymap), not centered. The scrim color is the regime ground at 60% (`--color-overlay` override in `jp-regime`), never a blur.
 74: 
 75: ### 7.1 WhoamiSheet
 76: 
 77: Heading `<h2>` C-WHO-H. Lines in order: C-WHO-EDU, C-WHO-PLACE, then the three roles as a `<ul>` with no bullets (each `<li>` one line), then a links row: C-WHO-LINK-CB, C-WHO-LINK-GH, C-WHO-LINK-LI (Astryx `Link`, `isExternalLink`).
 78: 
 79: **Field-quiet (W1).** Whoami shows no brand mark. While the sheet is open over any scene, the field stays quiet per `scene.whoami.gradient` (lead ink, tail field, tailLength 0.5, static light, gain 0.5): no hero mark appears, no ring grows, and no logo looms. The server field hero (S05) is the quiet counterpart, not a mark.
 80: 
 81: ### 7.2 ContactSheet
 82: 
 83: Heading `<h2>` C-CON-H. The address C-CON-EMAIL as selectable text in `--text-large` weight 700 (`user-select: all`). A button row: `Button variant="primary" label=C-CON-COPY` and `Button variant="secondary" label=C-CON-MAIL href="mailto:justinf0829@gmail.com"`. Then C-CON-LINK-LI.
 84: 
 85: Copy behavior: `copyText` from `src/lib/copyText.ts` (verbatim from repo `spec/standard-implementations.md`). On `true`: `showToast({body: C-TOAST-OK, type: 'info', uniqueID: 'copy'})`. On `false`: select the address text node with a `Range`, `showToast({body: C-TOAST-FAIL, type: 'error', uniqueID: 'copy'})`. Never silent.
 86: 
 87: ### 7.3 CopyConfirm
 88: 
 89: Astryx `Toast` through `useToast`, stock anatomy, colors from tokens. Enter animation is Astryx's own (verify it is at most 225ms; if longer, override the duration via the theme `components.toast` target).
 90: 
 91: **Toast layer decision (W1).** Toasts render in the top-most open layer (DESIGN.md section 7). W0 confirmed the cause is top-layer paint order: the Contact sheet dialog opened later paints above the earlier-shown toast popover because BottomSheet never registers a modal outlet. No `layer.*` tokens are added: the W2 fix is portal rehost on modal open or outlet exposure for the sheet, and W2 decides between those two. Tests T-TOAST-1 (elementFromPoint finds the toast over the open sheet), T-TOAST-2 (not inert, announced), and T-TOAST-3 (activeElement unchanged) cover it.
 92: 
 93: ## 8. Live region
 94: 
 95: `.jp-live` is visually hidden (the standard clip pattern). S03 writes the new scene's H1 text into it after travel. Nothing else writes to it.
 96: 
 97: ## 9. Motion summary
 98: 
 99: | Element | Trigger | Animation |
100: |---|---|---|
101: | Keymap item | hover, focus, active | color, 150ms, ease |
102: | Readout | travel | out: opacity 150ms ease; in: opacity and translateY 8px, 300ms expo-out, 400ms after input |
103: | Wordmark | travel | same as readout |
104: | BottomSheet | open, close | Astryx built-in; must be at most 300ms (check with `getAnimations()`), opacity and transform only |
105: | Toast | show | Astryx built-in, at most 225ms |
106: | Everything | reduced motion | opacity only, 150ms |
107: 
108: ## 10. Kill criteria
109: 
110: - K-S04-1: the keymap wraps, truncates, or shows a hamburger at any width from 320 to 2560px.
111: - K-S04-2: any chrome element uses a shadow, gradient, blur, or a radius other than 0, 2px or 4px.
112: - K-S04-3: any hover changes size or position.
113: - K-S04-4: a sheet opened by keyboard does not return focus to its keymap button on close.
114: - K-S04-5: the page scrolls (document `scrollHeight > innerHeight`) at any route on any tested viewport.
115: - K-S04-6: any visible text is not from S09.
116: 
117: ## 11. Receipts
118: 
119: | Test | Claim | How |
120: |---|---|---|
121: | T-S04-1 | No wrap | Playwright at 320, 360, 390, 768, 1024, 1440, 2560 wide: keymap `getBoundingClientRect().height` equals token height and all five items share one `top` |
122: | T-S04-2 | Targets | coarse emulation: each keymap item and sheet control at least 44 by 44 |
123: | T-S04-3 | axe clean | `@axe-core/playwright` on each route with each sheet closed and open: zero violations |
124: | T-S04-4 | Copy paths | grant clipboard permission: toast C-TOAST-OK; deny it and stub `execCommand` to false: toast C-TOAST-FAIL and the address is the current selection |
125: | T-S04-5 | Focus return | open each sheet with Enter on its keymap button, close with Escape, `activeElement` is that button |
126: | T-S04-6 | Keyboard only | Tab order: wordmark (when shown), readout links, OfferButton or marker twins when present, keymap items in order |
127: | T-S04-7 | Sheet timing | sum of animation durations from `getAnimations()` on open at most 300ms |
128: 
129: ## 12. Unknowns
130: 
131: U-S04-1: whether BottomSheet's built-in open animation exceeds 300ms; resolved in T-S04-7, fixed by theme override if needed.
132: 
133: ## 13. Changelog
134: 
135: - 1.1.0, 2026-11-01: W1 name clear zone (section 5) with titles sized to fit, readout clearance below the zone, toast layer decision (section 7.3, no layer.* tokens), whoami field-quiet (section 7.1). No keymap, sheet, or copy change.
136: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
</file>

<file path="spec/astryx/specs/S06-scene-leaflink.md">
  1: # S06 Scene: LeafLink, mutual aid (`/leaflink`)
  2: 
  3: Version 1.1.0, 2026-11-01. Depends on: S02, S03, S04, S09, DESIGN `world.leaflink`, `world.camera.leaflink`. Blocks: S10 poster, S11 scene gates.
  4: 
  5: ## 1. Purpose
  6: 
  7: Show what LeafLink does by doing it. The lanes fall flat and become the streets of a neighborhood drawn in ink on paper. Requests (rings) and offers (dots) land on the map at an irregular, human cadence; when a request and an offer are near each other, a route is traced along the streets between them and both pins get a soft halo: matched. The visitor can drop their own offer and watch the nearest open request route to it. The readout gives the facts: mutual aid, week-long hackathon, team of four, 2nd place, the stack, the code.
  8: 
  9: ## 2. Visual identity
 10: 
 11: - Ground: paper `#F3F0EA` (warm, hue 85). Everything else is ink `#0E3565` at different weights: local streets 1.2px at 30%, arterials 2.2px at 55%, routes 3px at 100%, pins solid. Halos are ink tint `#C9DCEA`.
 12: - This is the only light scene on the site. Its warmth comes from the warm paper against cool ink and from the timing, never from an orange.
 13: - Geometry: an irregular street grid (jittered lattice with dropped and diagonal streets) seen from a high three-quarter angle like a paper map on a table.
 14: - Motion: heartbeat cadence. Events at irregular intervals; each landing is a quick drop, each match a line drawn at constant speed. Nothing periodic.
 15: - Not a Leaflet clone: no tiles, no zoom buttons, no attribution box, no teardrop markers, no real geography, no place names.
 16: 
 17: ## 3. Layout
 18: 
 19: ### 3.1 Street generator (`src/world/layouts/leaflink.ts`, worker)
 20: 
 21: Use `rng(seed + 1 * 1000003)`.
 22: 
 23: 1. **Nodes.** Lattice `n = 33` by 33, pitch 1.25. Node `(i, j)` base position `((i - 16) * 1.25, 0, (j - 16) * 1.25)`. Jitter x and z each by `(r() * 2 - 1) * 0.28`. Nodes on an arterial row or column (`i % 8 == 0` or `j % 8 == 0`) get half jitter on the axis perpendicular to that arterial.
 24: 2. **Edges.** All horizontal `(i, j)-(i+1, j)` and vertical `(i, j)-(i, j+1)`: 2,112 edges. An edge is arterial if both endpoints share an arterial row (horizontal) or column (vertical).
 25: 3. **Drop.** Each non-arterial edge is dropped with probability 0.12. Then restore, in a seeded random order, any dropped edge whose endpoints are in different union-find components or whose endpoint would have degree 0. The final graph is connected.
 26: 4. **Diagonals.** For each cell `(i, j)` with `r() < 0.04`, add one diagonal: `(i, j)-(i+1, j+1)` if `r() < 0.5`, else `(i+1, j)-(i, j+1)`. Diagonals are local.
 27: 5. **Allocate exactly 6,144 lanes.** Edge lengths `L_e`, total `S`. Quota `q_e = 6144 * L_e / S`, `k_e = max(1, floor(q_e))`. If `sum k_e < 6144`, add 1 to the edges with the largest `q_e - floor(q_e)` until equal. If `sum k_e > 6144`, subtract 1 from edges with the largest `k_e` (never below 1) until equal. Assert equality.
 28: 6. **Emit.** Edge `e` from node `a` to node `b` with `k_e` lanes: lane `s` covers `t in [s / k_e, (s + 1) / k_e]`, 8 points evenly spaced on that interval, `y = 0`. Class 3 for arterial, else 2. Record per edge `{a, b, length, laneStart, laneCount, arterial}`; lanes of an edge are contiguous ids in the order `a` to `b`.
 29: 7. **Side data posted with the layout:** `nodes: Float32Array(x, z)`, `edges` as above, adjacency lists.
 30: 
 31: ### 3.2 Camera and framing
 32: 
 33: Fine: camera `(0, 30, 21)` looking at `(0, 0, 1.5)`, fov 34, no shift (the legend slip is opaque and may cover map). Coarse: `(0, 34, 18)`, fov 42, shift `+0.18` in y so the map's center rides above the bottom slip. Idle drift: pos and target translate together by `(3 sin(2 pi t / 40), 0, 2 sin(2 pi t / 40 * 0.7))`.
 34: 
 35: ### 3.3 Readout (variant `leaflink`, the legend slip)
 36: 
 37: C-LL-H1 (display-1), C-LL-P1, C-LL-FACTS, C-LL-STACK, C-LL-CODE (link), then a hairline rule, then C-LL-LEGEND with three inline glyphs drawn in CSS (a 10px ink ring, a 10px ink dot, a 16px by 3px ink bar) each before its word, then C-LL-SIM, then the `OfferButton` (C-LL-OFFER, Astryx `Button variant="secondary" size="sm"`). World region `aria-label` C-LL-ALT.
 38: 
 39: ### 3.4 Hero mark (W1)
 40: 
 41: The LeafLink hero is the project's brand mark, rendered by `BrandHero` (DESIGN.md sections 3.5, 6.8, 9) at the hero anchor inside the scene. Descriptor: kind mark, ring r, mark leaflink. W3 confirms whether `leaves.gif` or `logo.png` is the shipped mark; `logo.png` is also the reduced-motion PNG and the F1 fallback source. The hero holds exactly one mark: no second image shares the viewport.
 42: 
 43: Placement: outside the name clear zone (`zone.name.padPx`), outside the readout clear zone (the legend slip), and outside the keymap. Displayed size comes from `hero.sizePx` and `hero.minSizePx`, fitted inside the brand area cap (`brand.areaCap` 0.12 at 390x844) from the mark's aspect ratio; the cap governs, so a portrait mark displays narrower than a landscape mark. Gradient: `scene.leaflink.gradient` (lead ink, tail field, tailLength 1.0, radial light from the hero anchor; the ring brightens on each heartbeat, then decays). Passive motion: a small scale on each beat, peak `hero.beatScale` 1.015, continuous. Active motion: pointer or touch-drag tilts the plane within `hero.tiltMaxDeg` 8; a tap sends one ripple through the ring (150ms out, 700ms settle). Under reduced motion the hero shows the static PNG with an identity transform. The hero rectangle never overlaps the name zone, the readout zone, or the keymap (T-HERO-3).
 44: 
 45: ## 4. The simulation (`src/world/leaflink.ts`, main thread controller)
 46: 
 47: Deterministic: its own `rng(seed + 7)`. Same seed, same first minute of events (screenshot tests rely on it).
 48: 
 49: - **Spawn.** Next event after a uniform delay in `leaflinkEventIntervalMs` `[1000, 1600]`. Kind: request with probability `requestFraction` 0.55, else offer. Position: a random node at least 2 hops from every existing pin. Skip the spawn (reschedule) if `maxPins` 24 pins exist.
 50: - **Landing.** Pin scale from `dropScaleFrom` 1.6 to 1 and opacity 0 to 1 over `--duration-medium-min` (200ms), `easeSignature`. A ripple ring expands from 1x to `rippleScaleTo` 3x pin radius with alpha from 0.4 to 0 over `--jp-duration-ripple` (900ms), `easePlain`.
 51: - **Match.** After landing, find the nearest open pin of the opposite kind by Dijkstra on edge length, giving up beyond `matchMaxHops` 9 hops. If found and fewer than `maxMatches` 6 matches are in flight: convert the node path to a lane list (for each edge in order, its lanes in traversal order; reverse order and negative rate when the path runs `b` to `a`). For each lane: `start = tNow + arcBefore / routeSpeed`, `uPerSec = routeSpeed / laneLength` (signed), `fadeAt = tEnd + holdMs / 1000`, where `routeSpeed = 6` units per second and `tEnd` is when the last lane finishes. Call `setActivity(lanes, start, fadeAt, 1, uPerSec)`.
 52: - **Answered.** At `tEnd`, both pins grow an ink-tint halo disc from 0 to 2.2x pin radius over `--duration-medium` (300ms), `easeSignature`. Hold `holdMs` 4000. Then route (shader fade, `uFadeSec = fadeMs / 1000`), both pins and halos fade over `fadeMs` 600, `easePlain`, and are removed.
 53: - **Unmatched pins** stay until matched; if a pin is older than 20s it fades out like a matched one (keeps the map from silting up).
 54: 
 55: ### 4.1 Pins and halos (draw calls: pins 1, halos 1, ripples 1)
 56: 
 57: Each is an `InstancedMesh` of a unit quad lying on `y = 0.01`, with a tiny shader that sizes it in screen space: project the center, offset corners by `radiusPx * scale * uDpr` device pixels. Fragment draws an SDF: request ring (annulus inner 0.62, outer 1.0), offer dot (disc), halo (disc, ink tint), ripple (annulus inner 0.86, outer 1.0). Colors are ink and ink tint, premultiplied over, same blending as lanes. Draw order: halos, lanes (already drawn), ripples, pins (set `renderOrder`). Pin radius `pins.radiusPx` 7 CSS px fine, 8 coarse.
 58: 
 59: ## 5. Interaction
 60: 
 61: - **Drop an offer (pointer).** `pointerup` on the canvas region with total movement under 6 CSS px and duration under 500ms (a tap, not a drag): `screenToGround`, snap to the nearest node, place a visitor offer there (lands like any pin), then match it to the nearest open request with no hop limit. If no request is open, spawn one 3 to 6 hops away immediately (it lands first), then match. The response is guaranteed and starts within one frame.
 62: - **Drop an offer (keyboard and assistive tech).** `OfferButton` does the same at the node nearest the map center. Announce nothing extra; the legend explains the drawing.
 63: - Canvas region `pointer-events: auto` on this scene only where no DOM is on top; the legend slip and keymap take their own events.
 64: - The visitor's pins obey the same caps; if `maxMatches` is reached, the oldest match fades immediately to make room.
 65: 
 66: ## 6. Motion table
 67: 
 68: | Phase | What | Duration, easing, trigger |
 69: |---|---|---|
 70: | Entry (travel in) | Lanes flatten into streets; ground goes void (or field) to paper on plain ease; camera rises to the map pose. Simulation resumes at t = 700 with one event scheduled 300ms later so something lands soon. | 700ms expo-out; 700ms ease for ground |
 71: | Hero (morph in) | The deflection ring grows at the hero anchor during Gather; the mark looms in during Reveal (DESIGN.md section 6.7). On exit the mark recedes and the ring collapses. | Inside travel 700ms |
 72: | Hero (idle and active) | Passive beat scale, continuous. Tilt on pointer or touch-drag within 8 degrees; tap ripple through the ring. | Beat scale peak 1.015; ripple 150ms out, 700ms settle |
 73: | Idle | Spawns, landings, ripples, routes, halos, fades (section 4). Camera drift. | Heartbeat cadence |
 74: | Interaction | Tap or OfferButton (section 5). Button press state is Astryx default. | Response next frame |
 75: | Exit | Controller pauses: no new spawns; existing pins and routes freeze in place and fade with `uW.y`. On return, they resume. | With travel |
 76: | Reduced motion | Static map: place the first 6 pins and complete the first 3 matches of the seeded sequence instantly (routes fully drawn, halos full), no spawns, no ripples, no drift. Tap and OfferButton still work; routes appear fully drawn at once. | Static |
 77: 
 78: ## 7. Content
 79: 
 80: S09 IDs: C-TITLE-LEAFLINK, C-LL-H1, C-LL-P1, C-LL-FACTS, C-LL-STACK, C-LL-CODE, C-LL-LEGEND, C-LL-SIM, C-LL-OFFER, C-LL-ALT. The scene shows no text. Pins carry no labels, categories or names (LeafLink's real categories are UNKNOWN; inventing "groceries" or "ladder" would be fabricating product detail).
 81: 
 82: ## 8. Kill criteria
 83: 
 84: - K-LL-1: any count, stat, name, category, place name or label on the map.
 85: - K-LL-2: any orange, copper or warm accent hue (warmth must come from paper and timing).
 86: - K-LL-3: the map looks like a Leaflet or OpenStreetMap screenshot, or uses tiles or a marker icon.
 87: - K-LL-4: spawns are periodic (interval variance of the first 30 spawns is zero, or any two consecutive intervals are equal to the millisecond).
 88: - K-LL-5: a tap does not produce a route within 1 frame plus the landing time.
 89: - K-LL-6: the graph is disconnected (some tap can find no request).
 90: - K-LL-7: hue budget over `hueBudgetPct.leaflink` (10%).
 91: 
 92: ## 9. Receipts
 93: 
 94: | Test | Claim | How |
 95: |---|---|---|
 96: | T-LL-1 | Exactly 6,144 lanes, connected graph | worker unit test: lane total, union-find single component |
 97: | T-LL-2 | Deterministic first minute | record the first 20 events (kind, node, time) twice from fresh loads: identical |
 98: | T-LL-3 | Tap responds | Playwright taps the map center; within 300ms a route lane has activity start time at or before `now()` and a visitor pin exists |
 99: | T-LL-4 | Keyboard path | focus OfferButton, press Enter: same as T-LL-3 |
100: | T-LL-5 | Legend slip contrast | all slip text pairs pass (paper.text, paper.text2 on paper.raised are in the receipt table) |
101: | T-LL-6 | No labels in scene | the scene graph contains no troika `Text` while `uW.y > 0.99` |
102: | T-LL-7 | Hue budget | G-HUE: under `hueBudgetPct.leaflink` (10%) of pixels with OKLCH chroma above 0.04, after 30s of simulation |
103: 
104: ## 10. Unknowns
105: 
106: U-LL-1: LeafLink's real request and offer categories, UI and screenshots: UNKNOWN, not depicted. U-LL-2: resolved, Justin confirmed "mutual aid" on 2026-10-30 (S09 D-LEAFLINK-KIND). U-LL-3: whether LeafLink is still running: UNKNOWN; copy uses "Simulated feed" and never claims it is live now.
107: 
108: ## 11. Changelog
109: 
110: - 1.1.0, 2026-11-01: W1 hero section (3.4) and hero motion rows: brand mark at the hero anchor, placement outside the name, readout, and keymap zones, area cap governs display size, beat scale and tilt, reduced-motion static PNG. No map, pin, or copy change.
111: - 1.0.1, 2026-10-30: mutual aid confirmed by Justin.
112: - 1.0.0, 2026-10-30: initial.
